export const PROTOCOL_BODIES: Record<string, string> = {
  "01-where-you-actually-are": "## Welcome to Week One\n\nHere's the thing nobody tells you about getting faster: most cyclists who plateau aren't training too little. They're training without knowing where they actually are.\n\nYou know your FTP. You know your weekly hours. You probably know your weight to within a kilo. But ask the harder question — what is the real intensity distribution of your last four weeks, what is your honest sleep average, what is your actual available training time once you subtract the commute and the kids' bedtime — and the answer gets fuzzy fast.\n\nThat fuzziness is the problem. Every coaching decision you'll make over the next twelve weeks depends on a real picture of where you are right now. Not the version you'd present to a club mate. The honest one. This week is the audit.\n\n## Why this week matters more than the training week that follows\n\nWhen Professor Seiler walks through how he'd build a plan from scratch, he doesn't start with intervals. He doesn't even start with duration. He starts with a baseline of what the athlete is actually doing — how often they get out the door, what their week looks like, where the load sits. Everything builds from that.\n\nDan Lorang describes the same thing in different language. When he coaches an amateur, the first thing he does is sit them down with their calendar and ask them to be honest about the working week — not what they'd like the week to look like, but what it really looks like. He's blunt about the consequence of skipping that step: \"What people try in general is they get a training plan and try to fit it in somehow and then it just ends up in pure stress and sickness is just a consequence of it.\"\n\nSickness is the consequence of training a fictional version of your life. That's why this week comes before the architecture. The architecture has to be built around the real numbers, the real time, and the real you.\n\n## The Entry Assessment — what you're actually measuring\n\nThe Entry Assessment Workbook (in your downloads) covers six domains. Not because we love spreadsheets — because each domain is a place performance leaks if you don't know your starting point.\n\n**1. Power baseline.** A current FTP. If you have a recent test (within the last 8 weeks) and you trust the protocol, validate it with a 20-minute effort and use 95% of average. If it's older, retest. The protocol guidance is in the workbook — pick the one that fits your kit and your time. The number isn't the prize. The number is the reference point. If you're unsure which FTP test protocol to use, [Power Meter Training for Cyclists](/blog/power-meter-training-cyclists-how-to-use) walks through the options.\n\n**2. Real training load.** Pull your last four weeks of completed rides from your head unit, your training app, or wherever your data lives. Don't summarise. Don't average. List them — date, duration, average heart rate, perceived effort. We need the texture of the last month, not the headline.\n\n**3. Body composition baseline.** Weight on a normal scale, first thing in the morning, no clothing, after the bathroom. Estimated body fat from waist-to-hip ratio (the workbook has the simple table). Photos from the front, side, back. This is uncomfortable. It's also the only honest baseline. The scale moves around. Tape measures and pictures don't lie.\n\n**4. Sleep average.** Your honest seven-night average for the last fortnight. Not your idealised number. Not what your watch claims. Your real average — bedtime to wake-up, minus the time you were lying there scrolling. If you're below seven hours, write down why. We come back to this in Week 5.\n\n**5. Real available hours.** Block your calendar — the way Lorang describes for the riders he actually coaches. Work blocks. Commute. Family time. Sleep. Showers. The session that takes 90 minutes plus a 20-minute change and shower and a bit of cool-down conversation is not a 90-minute session. It's a two-hour block. Map your real available time. The number that comes out is almost always smaller than people thought, and that's fine — you're going to train inside reality.\n\n**6. The goal.** One primary 12-week goal. Two supporting goals. A single measurable target you'll come back to in Week 12. The \"One-Two-Twelve\" framework. We'll use the supporting goals as the structure, not the primary goal — because most riders write a primary goal that's emotional and a set of supporting goals that are actually trackable. We work with both.\n\n## Your intensity distribution — and why it's almost certainly wrong\n\nThis is the part most riders fight on the way through. Pull your last four weeks of rides and calculate, by minute, the percentage spent in three buckets:\n\n- **Green** — easy, conversational, heart rate flat after warm-up, leaves you with an appetite at the dinner table. This is what Seiler calls Zone 1 in his green-yellow-red model.\n- **Yellow** — sustained moderate work. Climbs you push a bit, group rides where you don't want to be the one off the back, threshold sessions that drift up.\n- **Red** — actual high-intensity intervals where the work is structured and the recovery is structured.\n\nThe polarised model — what almost every successful elite endurance program converges on — is roughly 80% green, very little yellow, the rest red.\n\nThe amateur model is, on average, something like 30–40% green, 40–60% yellow, and a smattering of red. Most age-group cyclists ride about 50% too hard when they think they're riding easy.\n\nDon't take that on faith. Calculate it for yourself this week. The number you produce becomes the single most important diagnostic in this entire course. If your yellow zone is bigger than your green zone, you don't need more training — you need different training. We build that in Week 2.\n\n## What you'll likely find — and why that's good news\n\nMost people running this audit honestly for the first time find some version of the following:\n\n- The intensity distribution is upside-down. The yellow zone is the biggest bucket.\n- The sleep average is below seven hours and they didn't realise.\n- The real available training time is meaningfully less than the time they'd been trying to schedule into.\n- They've been doing the same week for months — same routes, same group rides, same Tuesday session — without progression.\n- There's no recovery week in the last 12 weeks of training.\n\nIf three of those five describe you, you've already learned something more useful than another six weeks of effort. The reason your FTP isn't moving isn't that you don't train hard enough. It's that the training isn't shaped right for adaptation. That's a fixable problem. It's the entire reason this course exists.\n\n## Common mistakes to avoid this week\n\n**Re-writing history to look better.** The audit only works if it's honest. If you didn't ride three Tuesdays in a row because work blew up, write that down. The audit is for you, not for a coach you're trying to impress. The version of you in Week 12 is the audience. They need the truth.\n\n**Re-testing FTP and going too hard.** If you do test this week, treat it as a baseline, not a personal best. A bad test won't sink the course — but a wrecked recovery will eat into Week 2. If the test is questionable, take a recent number and revalidate at the end of the block.\n\n**Building the goal around someone else's calendar.** Your goal has to be yours. Not your buddy's gran fondo. Not a number a coach put on a plan three years ago. One primary goal, two supporting goals, one measurable target. Yours.\n\n**Treating the audit like admin.** The audit is the training this week. The reason coaches charge what they charge is that this is the part most riders skip and it's also the part everything else hangs off. Take it seriously.\n\n**Skipping the body composition baseline because it feels uncomfortable.** It's uncomfortable because it matters. We talk about race weight properly in Week 8. We need the Week 1 number to make Week 8 honest.\n\n## How this connects to Week 2\n\nNext week we build the training architecture. Not a generic plan. The architecture for *your* hours, *your* current load, and *your* goal. Everything in Week 2 is going to assume the audit is done — that we know your real available time, your current intensity distribution, and the leak we're trying to fix first.\n\nIf you only do one thing this week, do the audit. The video walks you through it. The workbook holds your hand through it. The community is there if you want to share what came up. By next Monday, you'll know exactly what you're working with — and you'll feel the relief of having a clear picture for the first time in a long time.\n\nWelcome to The Roadman Method. The map starts here.",
  "02-training-architecture": "## Where Week 1 left you, and where Week 2 takes you\n\nYou've done the audit. You know your intensity distribution. You know your real available hours. You know the leak. Now we build the structure that fixes it.\n\nMost riders have heard of polarised training. Some can quote the 80/20 number. A few can name Professor Seiler. But almost nobody actually rides a polarised week — because the structure they've been given was built around an idealised athlete, not the cyclist with a 7am Monday meeting and two kids at school by quarter to nine.\n\nThis module fixes that. We're going to take the science of polarised training and translate it into a weekly architecture that fits the hours you actually have. By the end of this week, you'll have a template for your week that you can run for the next six weeks without touching it — and start to feel what 80/20 actually feels like in your legs.\n\n## The three levers, in order\n\nSeiler has a framework for building a plan that's worth tattooing on the inside of your sunglasses. He calls it the three levers: frequency, duration, intensity — and the order matters.\n\n> \"I'm going to begin with frequency. All I care about is you get out the door a certain number of times a week. Six weeks later, now let's stretch one of those workouts, start using duration, that second lever. And after 12 weeks, now I'm going to introduce that magic buzzword intervals and intensity. But not before. And I think that's one of the most common mistakes that's made — we use the intensity lever almost from day one.\"\n\nRead that twice. Most amateurs flip the order. They start with intensity — Tuesday night threshold, Thursday VO2, group ride hammer-fest on Saturday — and never properly extend duration or stabilise frequency. The body doesn't get the substrate it needs to absorb the hard work.\n\nFor Week 2, your only job is the first lever: get out the door consistently, keep the easy work properly easy, and let one ride extend in duration. Intensity is the smallest part of the menu this week. We add more in later weeks.\n\n## What polarised actually means\n\nSeiler's research, replicated across rowers, runners, cross-country skiers and cyclists, found something striking in elite endurance training: the successful athletes weren't doing daily threshold work. They were either riding very easily — building mitochondria with low-stress duration — or doing structured high-intensity work with structured recovery. Almost nothing in the middle.\n\nThe middle is the trap. It's where the workload feels productive but produces less aerobic adaptation per minute than the easy work, and less peripheral and central adaptation than the hard work. Every minute you spend in the yellow zone is a minute that wasn't either deeply aerobic or properly stressful. The yellow zone is the busiest road to the smallest improvement.\n\nSo the architecture this week has two jobs: make the easy days truly easy, and make the one or two hard sessions truly hard. The middle gets emptied.\n\n## The Roadman week — what it looks like\n\nThe downloadable templates give you four versions of the week — 6, 8, 10 and 12 hours. The structure is the same; the duration scales. The shape:\n\n**The anchor: long Zone 2.** One ride per week that extends. Conversational pace. Heart rate flat after warm-up. Appetite at the end. Three to five hours depending on your bracket. This is the ride that builds the engine. If you only do one quality session this week, this is it.\n\n**The midweek quality session.** This week — a second Zone 2 ride, slightly shorter, on a route that lets you stay in green. Not a sweet-spot session. Not a tempo loop. We're proving you can run two easy rides in a week before we add intensity in Week 5.\n\n**Recovery / freshen-up rides.** Short. Flat. Active recovery, or absolute Zone 1 floor. These exist to keep frequency up without building load. Half an hour to 90 minutes.\n\n**Strength.** Two 30-minute sessions, but we don't introduce the protocol until Week 4. This week, leave the gym slot in the calendar but use it as recovery if needed.\n\n**Rest day.** One full day off the bike. Genuine rest. Not \"active recovery just in case.\" Adaptation needs space.\n\n## How to actually keep the easy days easy\n\nHere's where most riders fail this week, and it's a fixable mistake. They go out intending to ride easy, see a hill, and unconsciously push it. The heart rate creeps. Suddenly Tuesday's \"Zone 2\" ride is averaging 150bpm with a 10-minute spike to 165 because the grade was 6%.\n\nThree tools, in order of how well they work:\n\n**1. The Seiler four-test on every easy ride.** Are you having a conversation with the rider beside you (or talking out loud to yourself) without it feeling difficult? Is your heart rate flat after warm-up? Are you finished with the ride and have an appetite? Could you go straight to the dinner table? If yes to all four, you're in green. If any are no, you're in yellow.\n\n**2. A heart-rate ceiling alarm on your head unit.** Set it to your LT1 heart rate (the workbook has the calculation). When the alarm fires, you back off. No negotiation. The bike computer is the coach.\n\n**3. Permission to ride the easy day stupidly easy.** This is the one Alistair Brownlee makes the strongest case for. He says the difference between 280 watts and 220 watts of Zone 2 is meaningless — but the difference in fatigue cost is huge. \"You actually don't even really need to know where the top of Zone 2 is. You just need to know that you're way below it.\"\n\nWay below it. Not on the threshold of it. The amateur version of Zone 2 lives at the top of the zone and tips into yellow when the road tilts. The pro version sits in the middle of the zone and stays there. Borrow the pro version this week.\n\n## The session you skip is the one you don't recognise\n\nThere's a session most amateurs do every week and never name as a session: the group ride. It's \"social.\" It's \"fun.\" But heart rate data tells the truth — it's almost always a yellow-zone effort with bursts of red. It's not a recovery ride. It's not a Zone 2 ride. It's a quality session you weren't planning.\n\nYou don't have to give it up. You do have to count it. If your Saturday group ride is going to be three hours of mostly tempo with surges, that becomes your one quality session for the week and the rest of the week needs to flex around it. The mistake is to do the group ride AND a midweek threshold AND a long Z2 — three quality sessions in seven days, with no proper recovery, in a week with eight available hours. That's the polarised model thrown out the window.\n\nThis week, name the group ride if you do one. Add it to the audit. Treat it like a session, not free riding. We'll work with it.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Adding intensity because you don't trust easy.** The temptation is to throw a sweet-spot session into the week to \"make sure something hard is happening.\" Don't. The point of this week is to feel what proper polarised work feels like in the body. Threshold and VO2 come back in later modules.\n\n**Riding fasted to \"burn fat.\"** This is the next module. For now, fuel every ride. Skipping carbs to chase fat oxidation is one of the leaks we're plugging in Week 3.\n\n**Trying to hit every hour in the bracket.** If the template says 8 hours and you have 6, ride 6. Frequency over volume. Lorang again: build the plan around the real working week, not a fantasy version.\n\n**Letting the long ride drift to yellow.** The long ride is the most valuable session in the entire week and the easiest to ruin. Set the heart-rate ceiling. Stay under it. If your buddies surge, let them go. Wiggins used to ride the climb, not race his rivals — there's a reason.\n\n**Counting strength as a rest day.** If you do a strength session, count the load. Even before we introduce the proper protocol in Week 4, gym work is real load. Don't double-up gym + intervals on the same day.\n\n## The metric that matters this week\n\nBy Sunday night, look at your week and answer one question: what percentage of my riding time was in green?\n\nIf it's 70% or higher, the architecture is holding. If it's 50%, the easy days drifted. If it's 30%, you ignored the heart-rate ceiling and rode by feel — and feel will lie to you for another six weeks if you let it.\n\nThis number is the diagnostic for whether the architecture is working. Track it weekly. If it stays above 70% for the next four weeks, your aerobic engine is rebuilding under you, even on weeks where the legs feel a bit flat. That's how it's supposed to feel — quietly, not dramatically.\n\n## How this connects to Week 3\n\nNext week we move to nutrition. This isn't a coincidence. The polarised week we just built only works if it's properly fuelled — if you under-fuel the long ride, you'll bonk; if you under-fuel daily protein, you'll never recover from the work. The architecture is the skeleton. Nutrition is the blood. Week 3 puts the two together, and you'll feel the difference inside ten days.\n\nThe architecture you've built this week is the skeleton — [Sweet-Spot Training: How and When to Use It](/blog/sweet-spot-training-cycling-guide) explains where that intensity sits in the bigger picture when you're ready for it.\n\nLock the architecture this week. Run it honestly. We'll add fuel next week.",
  "03-fuelling-the-engine": "## The lie the cycling internet keeps telling\n\nThe cycling internet is going to tell you that weight loss is simple. Calories in versus calories out. Burn more than you eat. Ride more. Eat less. This advice is so outdated it's actually making you slower — and in many cases fatter — than the day you started taking it seriously.\n\nDr David Dunne — the performance nutritionist behind the Hexis fuelling system, who's spent his career working with World Tour riders and the kind of amateurs we serve in this community — put a question to me on the podcast that lands hard if you've spent years counting calories: what if losing weight is actually making you slower on the bike? His answer, across the whole conversation, is that most of us have been doing it backwards.\n\nThis module is the reset. Not a diet. Not a meal plan. A fuelling system — the same one that took me from 86 to 79 kilos in 12 weeks while eating more food than I'd ever eaten in my life. Power didn't drop. Energy went up. The 9pm fridge raids stopped. We're going to walk through why that worked when \"eat less ride more\" failed for years before it.\n\n## Why most cyclists are under-fuelled and don't know it\n\nDunne is precise about why amateurs carry excess weight despite training ten or twelve hours a week. The popular framing is that they're eating too much. The honest answer is more uncomfortable.\n\n> \"If somebody is carrying excess mass or they're deliberately wanting to reduce weight and they're not reducing weight, typically it's going to be because they're not in an energy deficit. But the equation itself is not just your exercise energy expenditure. Your body is going to burn a certain amount of energy at rest, then if we look at that — you're not resting all day — there needs to be an adjustment done based on the type of job you're working at.\"\n\nThe full energy equation has four components: rest, daily activity, training, and the cost of the food itself. If you're sedentary at a desk, jet-fuelling protein at every meal, and not training as hard as you think you are, you can absolutely be in surplus on what looks like a moderate intake. Equally — and this is the trap most serious cyclists fall into — you can chronically under-fuel by trying to \"save calories\" around training, which puts you in survival mode where the body refuses to release fat and starts protecting itself instead.\n\nDunne is direct about what happens when you under-fuel for months at a time:\n\n> \"You can cause this additional stress where you have significant hours of being in this chronic deficit where your body is not getting enough fuel to maintain normal physiological processes. Your body works off this law of preservation — if we don't have enough energy, we'll start to shut off other processes.\"\n\nWhat gets shut off? Recovery. Hormone production. Bone density. Sleep quality. Adaptation. Everything that turns training into improvement.\n\n## The decision tool — training to train, or training to perform?\n\nBefore we get to specific numbers, you need a decision framework you'll use every day for the rest of this course. Dunne gives it to us in one question:\n\n> \"The first thing I'd ask them to distinguish is: are you training to train — so are you trying to facilitate an adaptation — or are you really training to perform, where it is about going as fast as you can for as long as you can? Once we have that identified then we know the overall aim of the session.\"\n\nApply that on every ride this week.\n\n**Training to train** — most Zone 2 sessions, easy spins, structured aerobic work where the goal is to build the engine. Fuel adequately so the work is sustainable, but the carb-per-hour target is moderate (30–60g/h depending on duration).\n\n**Training to perform** — race-pace efforts, your highest-stakes group ride of the week, threshold or VO2 sessions, key event simulations. Fuel hard. 60–90g/h, sometimes more. The goal is to execute the work to the highest possible quality. Adaptation is the byproduct, not the target.\n\nMost amateurs reverse this. They under-fuel hard sessions because they want to \"stay lean\" and over-fuel easy sessions because they treat every ride the same. The result: the hard sessions are sub-quality and the easy sessions are over-loaded. Reverse it. Hard fuelled hard. Easy fuelled clean and adequate.\n\n## Daily fuelling — the architecture, not the diet\n\nForget calorie counting. We're going to organise your day around four simple anchors.\n\n**1. A real breakfast on training days.** This is the single biggest change for most riders in this course. A coffee and a banana is not breakfast. A proper plate — oats with milk, fruit and yoghurt, eggs and toast, whatever fits your taste — protein and carbs together, in the morning. If you train fasted because you've been told it builds fat oxidation, here's the trade: you'll bonk on the long ride sixty kilometres from home and hate your life for the next three hours, and the marginal fat-adaptation gain is dwarfed by the loss of training quality. Eat breakfast.\n\n**2. Protein front-loaded across the day.** A useful target — 0.4 grams of protein per kilo of body weight at four meals across the day. For an 80kg cyclist, that's roughly 32g protein per meal, four meals a day. Most riders we work with eat almost no protein at breakfast (toast and jam), modest at lunch (a salad), and a big lump at dinner. The body can't store protein. Spread it out.\n\n**3. Carbs matched to the day.** On hard training days, carbs are the centre of the plate — rice, pasta, potatoes, bread, fruit. On easy or off days, they reduce. Not to zero. Reduce. This is what Dr Dunne calls carbohydrate periodisation: your fuelling should adjust day by day and meal by meal in line with the demands of the activity.\n\n**4. A proper post-training meal within an hour of the long ride.** This is when the body is asking for protein and carbohydrate. Skip it and you've wasted half the long ride.\n\n## In-ride fuelling — the number you've been getting wrong\n\nHere's where the gap between amateur and pro practice is widest, and where the easiest gains live. World Tour riders routinely target 90–120 grams of carbohydrate per hour during racing, and some are now training their guts to tolerate up to 140g/h.\n\nMost amateurs come in around 30–60g/h on long rides — and many do nothing for the first ninety minutes because they're \"fasted training.\"\n\nAlistair Brownlee, no soft touch on training, says it cleanly:\n\n> \"There is no doubt that effective fuelling allows you to do more work, to train and then obviously allows you to race longer, faster, harder. Going from 'have a gel when you feel like it' to 'everyone probably can push that envelope up to 90 or even 120 grams an hour' — there's no doubt that allows people to do more work.\"\n\nThe targets for this week, by session type:\n\n- **Short Z2 (under 90 min):** 30–40g/h. Bottle with carb mix or one bar.\n- **Long Z2 (90 min–3hr):** 50–70g/h. A mix of bottle carbs, a banana or rice cake, half a bar.\n- **Long endurance (3hr+):** 70–90g/h. Carb mix in the bottle plus solid food on the hour.\n- **Quality / race-pace:** 80–120g/h. This is where you train your gut to handle the pro range. Build this gradually — start at 60g/h and add 10g/h every week or two.\n\nThe gut needs training. If you've been on 30g/h for years, jumping to 90 will produce a gastric event you'd rather not write home about. Work up to it.\n\n## Sarah Berry's free intervention\n\nDr Sarah Berry's work on personalised nutrition produces one of the most useful insights for cyclists: meal order changes glucose response without changing meal content.\n\n> Eat fibre, fat or protein BEFORE the carbohydrates. Same meal, lower glucose peak. Slows gastric emptying. Free intervention — food order doesn't change food content.\n\nTry it this week. Salad before pasta. Eggs before toast. Yoghurt before granola. Five seconds of behaviour change. Smoother energy through the morning, easier afternoon riding. This is not a fad — it's the cheapest performance intervention in the entire course.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Treating fuelling as restriction.** The whole point is the opposite. We're going to add food, especially around training. The leanness comes from training quality and consistency, not from cutting calories.\n\n**Skipping breakfast on training days.** This is the single biggest fuelling mistake we see. Stop.\n\n**Doing fasted \"fat-burner\" rides.** The fat-adaptation argument doesn't survive contact with the data. Fuel the work.\n\n**Front-loading carbs at dinner.** Most amateurs are carb-stacked at the wrong end of the day — light all day, big plate at 8pm. Move some of those carbs to breakfast and lunch. Sleep improves. Morning training improves.\n\n**Buying twelve different supplements before fixing breakfast.** Whole food first. The supplement aisle is the last 5%. Get the architecture right before you buy the protein powder.\n\n## How this connects to Week 4\n\nYou've now got the training week (Week 2) and the fuelling that supports it (Week 3). Next week we step off the bike — into the gym — for the third pillar of the system. The work you do off the bike is what protects the work you do on it, and for cyclists over 35, it's not optional. We make the case, give you the protocol, and show you how to fit it into the architecture without destroying your legs for the next ride.\n\nThe full breakdown of what World Tour nutritionists prescribe — carb targets, protein timing, real food versus supplements — is in [Cycling Nutrition: What World Tour Nutritionists Actually Recommend](/blog/cycling-nutrition-world-tour-nutritionists).\n\nRun the fuelling framework hard this week. Eat breakfast. Hit your carb-per-hour target. Front-load protein. By Sunday, you'll feel the difference. By Week 5, you'll wonder why anyone trains any other way.",
  "04-strength-that-transfers": "## Why we're talking about the gym in Week 4\n\nYou're three weeks in. The training architecture is shaping up. The fuelling has clicked. And now I'm telling you to spend an hour a week off the bike, in the gym, lifting weights. If your reaction is some version of \"I came here to ride faster, not look like a CrossFitter\" — fair. Let me walk you through why every World Tour coach we've had on the podcast prescribes some version of this work, and why for cyclists past 35 it's not optional.\n\nThe first time strength training came up in the Roadman world properly was during a long conversation with Derek Teel, who has spent his career working with cyclists in the gym. He was unequivocal:\n\n> \"There's the obvious answer which is to increase your force production. But it's pretty undeniable at this point that strength training benefits just about everything on the bike with the exception of your VO2 max. Even longer efforts have been shown to be improved by strength training.\"\n\nEven longer efforts. That's the line that should make you sit up if you've been resisting. Strength work doesn't just help you sprint or finish a punchy climb. It improves cycling efficiency — the watts you cost per heartbeat — across the entire intensity range. And a meta-analysis in the European Journal of Applied Physiology pooled 17 studies covering 262 cyclists and confirmed the picture: heavy strength training significantly improves cycling efficiency, anaerobic power, and time-trial performance, with no negative effect on VO2 max.\n\nThat's the evidence base. Now let's talk about why this matters more after 40 than at any other point in your cycling life.\n\n## The masters case for the gym\n\nAfter the age of 40, you lose roughly 8% of your muscle mass per decade. After 70, the rate nearly doubles to about 15% per decade. The fibres you lose fastest are the type 2 fibres — the ones responsible for explosive power, surges, the moment you stand up to close a gap on a climb.\n\nIf you do nothing, those fibres atrophy. Your sprints get worse. Your ability to respond to a kick on a group ride disappears. Your \"good day\" power numbers drift downward year on year, and you assume that's just age. It isn't. Some of it is. Most of it is detraining the type 2 system you stopped recruiting.\n\nThe solution isn't more zone 2. The solution isn't another set of carbon wheels. It's two strength sessions a week, year-round, lifting heavy enough to recruit type 2 fibres. That's the Roadman position, and it's not a controversial one — it's where every coach we've talked to over the last 12 months lands when the conversation turns to athletes over 35.\n\n## The trade-off — 60 minutes off the bike, more gain\n\nTime-crunched cyclists often resist the gym because they think they don't have the hours. Derek Teel makes the case directly:\n\n> \"Even if you were to cut 60 minutes out total of your riding per week and you dedicated that to two 30-minute strength training sessions spread throughout the week, I would be shocked if you did not feel significantly better opposed to just doing a little bit more of the same.\"\n\nTrade an hour of bike volume — most likely an hour from the yellow zone, where it wasn't doing much for you anyway — for an hour of strength work. Two 30-minute sessions. That's the entire commitment.\n\nIf you've been resisting the gym because you thought it would cost you on the bike, the trade is favourable. Better engine, less injury risk, more durable through the season.\n\n## The Roadman programme — what you actually do\n\nThe S&C Manual in your downloads has the full programme with photos, sets, reps and progressions across three phases — Foundation, Build, Maintain. We're keeping the structure compatible with cyclists in the 35–55 age bracket, which means we steer clear of the heaviest barbell movements that carry injury risk on imperfect form. Two principles:\n\n**Principle 1: Cycling-specific transfer.** Every exercise is selected because it transfers to power output on the bike — primarily through the posterior chain (glutes, hamstrings, back) and the trunk. We don't waste sessions on bench press and bicep curls.\n\n**Principle 2: Form before load.** Heavy enough to drive adaptation, light enough that you can hold form for every rep. If you can't hold form, the weight is too high. If you can do 12 reps comfortably, the weight is too low. The sweet spot for cycling-specific strength is 5–8 reps per set, near maximal effort, with full recovery between sets.\n\nThe Foundation Phase circuit — Week 4 to Week 8 — uses dumbbell, kettlebell, and bodyweight movements you can do at home or in any commercial gym. The exercises:\n\n- **Reverse lunge, dumbbells in hand** — single-leg, posterior chain bias, transfers directly to pedalling force without the load and balance demands of a back squat\n- **Dumbbell hip thrust or single-leg hip thrust** — the glute exercise most cyclists are missing\n- **Step-ups, weighted** — single-leg power, mimics the climbing action\n- **Single-leg dumbbell hip hinge** — hamstring development that fixes the compensation pattern Derek Teel highlighted, controlled and low-load\n- **Push-ups, progressive** — upper body and core integration without barbell pressing\n- **Pallof press / anti-rotation** — trunk stability that protects the lower back on long rides\n- **Dead bug** — core activation that addresses the lower-back compensation most cyclists feel after 90 minutes in the saddle\n\nThree to four sets per exercise. Five to eight reps. Full recovery — two to three minutes between heavy sets. The session takes 30 minutes if you stay focused.\n\n## Why the hamstrings matter (and why your back keeps complaining)\n\nTwo specific compensation patterns Derek Teel calls out, both of which most cyclists in this audience will recognise.\n\n**The hamstring leak.** Cyclists are dominantly quad-driven. Years of pedalling without correcting it means the hamstrings underperform. The hamstrings should pull through the back of the pedal stroke — and when they don't, your quads work harder for the same power.\n\n> \"If you don't have your hamstrings supporting them a little bit and working a little bit you're not going to be getting as much out of your legs as you should. You can train it in a very controlled place like the gym where you put yourself in positions where it's almost impossible for your hamstrings not to be the primary mover.\"\n\nThat's why the single-leg hip hinge and hip thrust work is non-negotiable in the Foundation Phase. They train the back of the leg in positions where the hamstrings can't hide — and they do it without the high-load spinal compression of barbell movements that aren't worth the risk for cyclists in this age bracket.\n\n**The lower-back compensation.** Cyclists who feel their lower back light up after 90 minutes are almost always running an anti-pattern: weak abs, weak glutes, lower back trying to stabilise everything. Teel again:\n\n> \"If your lower back is getting fired up and it's compensating for what your core should be doing — it's trying to stabilise and work on overdrive because your abs are so inactive or you have no glute engagement. These are all the muscles that should be firing to protect your low back. How would you correct that on the bike? You really can't.\"\n\nYou can't correct it on the bike — you correct it in the gym. Pallof press, dead bugs, hip thrusts. The back stops complaining within four to six weeks.\n\n## How to fit the sessions into the week\n\nTwo 30-minute sessions, spaced 72 hours apart. The simplest schedule:\n\n- **Tuesday strength.** Light ride day or rest day. Strength session in the morning or early evening.\n- **Friday strength.** Pair with an easy spin or full rest. Saturday's long ride still works — you're 18 hours out.\n- **Avoid:** strength the day before a quality session, strength on the same day as the long ride, two strength sessions back-to-back.\n\nThe 24 hours after a strength session, your legs may feel heavy. That's not a sign the session was wrong. That's the work. Don't try to do a quality session on top of it. By 48 hours later, the legs are back. By 72, you've adapted.\n\n## What strength training costs you that doesn't show up in TrainingPeaks\n\nWorth flagging — Derek Teel has called it out, and it's important.\n\n> \"You just put so much damage on your system that you're going to have to recover from that you could feel for multiple days from those three reps. You would never ever get credit for a TSS score from something like that.\"\n\nA heavy strength session won't show up as a meaningful TSS number. But it absolutely costs you recovery. Don't trust TrainingPeaks alone — your fatigue score will say you're fresh when your legs are still cooked from Tuesday's hip thrusts. Subjective check matters.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Going too heavy in week one.** First session, you don't know what's going to break you. Start with weights you can confidently complete every rep with form. Add load in week two and three. By week four of the Foundation Phase, you'll be heavy enough.\n\n**Pairing strength with quality cycling.** Don't put strength the day before a hard interval session. Don't put it the day of a long ride. We schedule for 72 hours between strength and key bike sessions.\n\n**Treating it as cardio.** This is not a HIIT session. The rest periods between sets are the work — that's how you recruit type 2 fibres. If you're sweating like you just ran a 5k, you're going too light too fast.\n\n**Skipping the protein meal afterwards.** 30g of protein within 90 minutes. This is when the muscle adaptation is laid down. Skip it and you've left half the gain on the gym floor.\n\n**Quitting after three weeks.** Strength gains in cycling come around weeks 4–8 of consistent work. If you bail after three weeks, you got the fatigue without the adaptation. Stay with it.\n\n## How this connects to Week 5\n\nYou're now training, fuelling, and strengthening with intent. The piece most cyclists sabotage next is recovery — not because they're lazy, but because they feel guilty about resting and fill the rest day with \"easy\" rides that aren't easy enough. Next week we make the case for recovery as a performance multiplier, not an afterthought, and give you the framework to actually let the work absorb.\n\nFor the full evidence base on why the gym transfers to the bike — including the protocols most cyclists get wrong — [Strength Training for Cyclists: The Complete Guide](/blog/strength-training-cyclists-complete-guide) covers it in depth.\n\nThis is also when you'll start to feel the cumulative effect of the system. Four weeks in, things start to compound. Stay with it.",
  "05-recovery-multiplier": "## The hardest sell in cycling\n\nHere's the thing nobody tells you about getting faster: it doesn't happen during training. It happens between sessions. The training is the stimulus. The improvement is laid down in recovery.\n\nThat sentence is easy to nod at and impossible to live by. Most serious cyclists in this community are sabotaging their own adaptation by never truly recovering — not because they're lazy, but because they feel guilty about rest days and fill them with \"easy\" rides that aren't easy enough. Or because their idea of recovery is buying another gadget. Or because life stress is so high that the body is already running near capacity before training is added on top.\n\nThis week we change that. Not with a cold plunge. Not with a massage gun. With the cheapest, most powerful recovery intervention in the entire archive of this podcast — one that shows up across every single conversation with elite coaches and physiologists when the tape stops rolling and they get honest about what actually moves the needle.\n\n## The single most powerful recovery insight in the archive\n\nWhen Professor Seiler talks about recovery, he doesn't talk about supplements or recovery boots or hyperbaric chambers. He tells a story about Gustav Iden and Kristian Blummenfelt's coaches — two of the best triathletes on the planet at the time:\n\n> \"Those two guys said look, we don't use any of these recovery modalities. The only one we use is sleep. And they said that we found out that if we avoid the massage and the cold plunge, we have more time to sleep. And sleep is our recovery weapon. That's just about as old school as you can get but that's physiology — if we go back a thousand years we didn't have massage pistols and cold plunges, but sleep has been there.\"\n\nSleep is the recovery tool. Everything else is rounding error. If you only do one thing in this module, sort your sleep.\n\nThat sentence is going to feel inadequate. I know — because every time I say it on the podcast, the email replies are some version of \"but what about the cold plunge / sauna / recovery boots / glutamine / red-light therapy\". The honest answer is that those are second-order interventions on a foundation of seven and a half to eight hours of consistent sleep. If the foundation isn't there, the interventions are marketing.\n\nSeiler frames it the way a physiologist would:\n\n> \"Doing things right involves training in an ordinary way with great consistency — and amazingly, that prepares us for extraordinary performances.\"\n\nRecovery is boring, daily, and compounds. That's why it works. That's also why it gets neglected — there's nothing to post about a normal Tuesday night where you went to bed at 10:30pm.\n\n## What sleep actually does for the cyclist\n\nThree things, and all of them matter.\n\n**1. Adaptation consolidation.** Growth hormone release peaks in deep sleep. The repair work that turns yesterday's training stimulus into tomorrow's higher-performing tissue happens between roughly 11pm and 3am for most people. If you're up at 1am scrolling, you're missing the window.\n\n**2. Glycogen restoration.** Sleep is when your liver and muscles refill the carbohydrate stores you depleted on the bike. Bad sleep, slow refill. Slow refill, bad ride the next day.\n\n**3. Decision quality.** Tired brains make bad coaching decisions. Tired brains do the planned threshold session even though the legs are wrecked. Tired brains skip strength. Tired brains snack on rubbish at 9pm. Sleep doesn't just recover the body — it fixes the next-day choices.\n\nDr Sarah Berry's research, which we covered in the nutrition module, also closes a loop here: the same breakfast after a bad night's sleep produces a higher glucose peak than after a good night. Sleep is a nutrition variable. Recovery is a training variable. They're all one system.\n\n## The readiness check — five minutes every morning\n\nMost cyclists have heard of HRV. Fewer use it well. The piece that matters more than the absolute number is the trend, and the trend is something you can reproduce with a forty-second morning routine.\n\nEvery morning, before coffee, before phone, before anything, log four numbers in the readiness template:\n\n1. **Hours of sleep last night** (real, not what your watch says you should have got)\n2. **Subjective sleep quality** (1–10)\n3. **Resting heart rate** (or HRV if you have a strap)\n4. **Subjective leg feel** (1–10, how the legs feel in the first minute of standing up)\n\nAdd a single sentence about life stress.\n\nRun this for seven days and you'll start to see your own patterns. Most people in this audience find: bad sleep → high RHR → poor leg feel → sub-quality session that day. That's the pattern Lorang describes when he talks about the 50/50 split between data and subjective feedback:\n\n> \"It could be that two athletes saying 'I'm tired' but it means something different. The scale of saying 'I'm tired' is different from athlete to athlete. This is something where you have to make some kind of calibration.\"\n\nYou're calibrating yourself this week. By Sunday, you'll have a reference baseline. By Week 8, you'll be making real-time decisions on training quality from your morning numbers.\n\n## When to skip the session — the framework that gives you permission\n\nThe hardest part of recovery is admitting when to back off. Lorang has the cleanest decision tool I've heard from any coach:\n\n> \"If you are doing these intervals and you can stay in that frame, you continue. But if you drop in the first two intervals already — the quality at that point — then we skip and you just make an easy run. And we reset the program. The athlete is then sure: okay, it's fine that I'm tired. It's fine if I don't have my personal best session today.\"\n\nTwo intervals. If the first two are off the prescribed power or RPE by more than ~5%, you're not having an off day, you're having a wrong day for the session. Switch to easy. Don't grind through. Lorang's exact framing: it's not weakness, it's intelligent load management.\n\n## Stress is training load\n\nThis is where most amateurs get blindsided. Derek Teel has been honest about it:\n\n> \"I have three kids under four right now. I'm running my own business. There's days I leave and I'm so tense that I just need to breathe, I need to regulate my heart rate. I can't even think about having an effort right now. There's no data to quantify it necessarily. But this is why it's so important to refer to data but to also be introspective and to really try and feel your body.\"\n\nLife stress is real training load. Work pressure, parenting, financial worry, relationship tension — they all run through the same sympathetic nervous system that hard intervals run through. Stack a high-stress week on top of a hard training week and your body responds to the combined load, not the cycling load.\n\nLorang is direct with the riders he coaches: share the cycling plan with the people around you, build training around real life, and when stress spikes, lower the training load. Don't just power through. The body keeps a single ledger.\n\nThis week, audit the three biggest non-cycling stressors in your life. Map them onto the training calendar. If quality sessions are landing on the highest-stress days, move them. The schedule has to flex.\n\n## What an actual recovery week looks like\n\nWe're 5 weeks in. Most plans would have you doing a recovery week around now — and most riders do it badly. They drop volume by 30%, keep all the intensity, and call it a recovery week.\n\nA real recovery week:\n\n- Volume drops by 30–50%\n- Intensity drops to almost zero — one short opener at the end of the week, optional\n- Sleep is protected harder than usual\n- Strength: one short session at 50% load, not two\n- Long ride is shortened, not skipped\n- One genuine rest day with no exercise\n\nThis week is your first chance to run one. It's the right time. You've done four weeks of solid building. Take it. Resist the urge to \"just add\" one more quality session. Trust the process.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Filling the rest day with an easy ride.** Real rest is full off. Save the easy spin for tomorrow. The body needs a 24-hour window to do the deep adaptation work.\n\n**Treating recovery as something you do after training breaks you.** Recovery is something you do continuously — sleep, nutrition, stress management — so training never breaks you in the first place.\n\n**Buying a recovery gadget instead of fixing sleep.** If you're below 7.5 hours of sleep on average, no gadget will help. Fix the bedroom, the bedtime, and the alcohol first.\n\n**Skipping the readiness check.** Five minutes a day. The data you collect this week is the foundation for every smart training decision you'll make in the back half of the course.\n\n**Assuming low motivation means you're being lazy.** Persistent low motivation is a recovery flag. Your body is telling you something. Listen.\n\n**Sleeping under 7 hours and complaining the training plan isn't working.** It's not the plan. It's the sleep. Fix the sleep.\n\n## How this connects to Week 6\n\nYou're at the halfway point of the course. Five weeks of architecture, fuelling, strength, and recovery. Next week is the mid-course pivot — a check-in against your Week 1 baseline, a recalibration if needed, and a step away from the science to talk about something every training plan ignores: the craft. The accumulated wisdom of how to ride well that doesn't fit in a spreadsheet. Welcome to le metier.\n\nFor the full evidence base behind World Tour recovery protocols — what the pros actually do versus what gets sold in recovery ads — [Recovery for Cyclists: World Tour Protocols](/blog/recovery-for-cyclists-world-tour-protocols) has the breakdown.\n\nFor this week, sleep. Run the readiness check. Take the off-day seriously. By the end of the week, you should feel different — not more tired, but more rebuilt. That's adaptation working. That's recovery doing its job.",
  "06-le-metier": "## Why we stop here\n\nYou're at the halfway mark. Five weeks of architecture, fuelling, strength, and recovery — the science of getting faster. Now we step away from the science for a week, because cycling isn't only science. The French call it *le metier* — the craft. The accumulated wisdom of how to ride well that doesn't fit in a spreadsheet.\n\nThis module does three things. It runs a mid-course progress check so you can see what six weeks of disciplined work has actually produced. It teaches the pacing and positioning skills that turn fitness into performance. And it reconnects you to why you're doing any of this in the first place — because if you've forgotten why you ride, the next six weeks will feel like a chore instead of what they should feel like, which is a build toward something you actually want.\n\n## The mid-course check — what to measure, what to ignore\n\nBefore we get to craft, we measure. Open the Mid-Course Progress Check Worksheet — same six domains as your Week 1 audit:\n\n1. **Power baseline.** A short retest, or validation if you have honest data from the last two weeks of training.\n2. **Real training load.** Pull the last six weeks. What's your intensity distribution actually been? Compare it to your Week 1 distribution.\n3. **Body composition.** Same scale, same conditions, same time of day. Tape measure on the waist. Optional progress photos.\n4. **Sleep average.** Seven nights of honest data from the readiness check you started in Week 5.\n5. **Subjective.** Energy, mood, motivation, leg feel — same 1–10 scales as Week 1.\n6. **Goal progress.** Are you on track for your Week 12 target? Ahead? Behind?\n\nWrite the numbers down. Don't massage them. The Roadman Method works because the numbers are honest. If your Week 6 power is the same as Week 1, that's important information. If it's up by 15 watts, that's also important information. We treat both seriously.\n\n## What the data usually shows at Week 6\n\nIn our experience working with riders through this system, what you're likely to see at the mid-course mark:\n\n- **Intensity distribution has shifted.** Your green-zone percentage is usually up by a meaningful chunk — sometimes from 35% to 70% over six weeks. This is the foundation. The aerobic engine is rebuilding even if power hasn't moved yet.\n- **Power is flat or up modestly.** Six weeks isn't long enough to see big FTP jumps in most riders, and that's fine. The architecture is what's been laid down. The performance gains come in the back half.\n- **Body composition has moved.** Two to three kilos for most riders, even though we haven't been chasing weight. The fuelling change does a lot of the work without pain.\n- **Sleep is up.** Whatever the average was in Week 1, it's usually up by 30–60 minutes by Week 6 because the readiness work has surfaced where the leak was.\n- **Energy and mood are up.** Subjective scales drift in the right direction. Riders feel less hammered between sessions.\n\nIf your data shows three of those five, the system is working. If it doesn't, the recalibration tools are in the workbook — and the Not Done Yet community is where you post for diagnosis. Don't suffer in silence.\n\n## When to recalibrate the 12-week goal\n\nMost goals set in Week 1 hold up at Week 6. Some don't. Two situations where you should recalibrate:\n\n**The goal was too easy and you're already there.** Some riders set conservative goals because they didn't know what was possible. If you've already hit your Week 12 target by Week 6, raise it. Pick the next stretch goal that scares you a bit.\n\n**The goal was wrong-shaped.** A few riders set goals that turn out to be the wrong type of target — they thought they wanted FTP +20w but what they really wanted was to drop a kilo and ride a faster sportive. The data will reveal it. If you set a power goal but the joy is coming from longer endurance rides, swap it.\n\nWhat you don't do is recalibrate downward because you're behind schedule. Behind is a signal to look at the inputs (sleep, fuelling, intensity discipline, life stress), not to lower the bar.\n\n## Now to the craft\n\nLachlan Morton has been on the podcast more times than almost anyone, and the conversations always come back to the same theme: cycling isn't only about going fast. He left the World Tour at the height of his ability for a reason — and the reason is the heart of what *le metier* actually means.\n\n> \"I joined the World Tour when I was 19 or 20 with Garmin. Up until that point, all my cycling goals were based on winning road races and trying to be a professional in Europe. And then I sort of achieved that early and then just wasn't feeling super fulfilled with that pursuit.\"\n\nHe chased the highest version of competitive performance and got there, and the achievement felt hollow. Not because the racing wasn't good — it was — but because performance without meaning is a thin reward. So he pivoted. Alt-racing. Bike-packing. Long solo days where the meaning is in the ride, not the result.\n\nThe lesson for the cyclist sitting at home with a Premium subscription and a TrainingPeaks plan isn't \"quit everything and go bike-packing.\" It's the question Morton asks himself constantly:\n\n> \"Sometimes stepping back and looking around and being like, ah, I'm not actually enjoying this. I'm pursuing this for the wrong reason. What's something that would be exciting for me and motivating for me and going to get me out stoked on my bike every day?\"\n\nIf you're not enjoying it, something needs to change. Not the volume. Not the intensity. The relationship to the bike. This week is a deliberate pause to look at that.\n\n## Pacing — the most undertrained skill in amateur cycling\n\nOnto the practical craft. The single biggest tactical leak in amateur racing and sportives isn't fitness — it's pacing.\n\nThe pattern is depressingly consistent. The road tilts up at the bottom of a climb, the heart rate jumps, the rider stays with the front group out of pride, blows up halfway up, and watches the wheel they were on drift away. Five minutes earlier they were strong. Now they're doing limit-state damage control to the top.\n\nWiggins, when he was at his climbing best, didn't race the climb against his rivals. He rode the climb. His own pace, his own threshold, his own breathing. When you're a level above the field, you can do that. When you're not, you have to make the same decision deliberately, every climb, every time.\n\nThe Roadman Climbing Checklist (in your downloads) gives you the five fixable mistakes — pacing, weight-to-power, cadence, position, and the mental game. Of those five, pacing is the one that gives the biggest returns the soonest. Get it right and you can finish ten percent faster on the same fitness.\n\nThree rules for pacing this week:\n\n**1. Start the climb at 90% of your sustainable effort, not 110%.** Most amateurs hit the climb 20% over their sustainable wattage in the first minute. The first minute is where they wreck themselves. Start under, build into it, finish strong.\n\n**2. Pace by RPE, not by what the wheel in front is doing.** If the wheel goes a watt or two above your sustainable, let it go. You'll catch them when they explode at the top. This requires confidence — see the mental game module in Week 10.\n\n**3. Use a target heart rate ceiling on long climbs.** Set the alarm. If the heart rate goes above the ceiling, you back off. The bike is the coach.\n\nFor a full breakdown of the five pacing mistakes that cost cyclists the most time on climbs — with fixes — [Cycling Pacing Strategy: How to Ride Long Climbs Faster](/blog/cycling-pacing-strategy-long-climbs) is worth reading before your next hilly event.\n\n## Group riding — read the wheel, don't fight it\n\nPositioning in a group is its own craft, and most amateurs ride at the back because they're nervous. The energy cost of being at the back is significant — you're surfing surges and accelerations all day. The cost of being in the middle of a tight group, on the right wheel, is far less.\n\nThree skills to practise this week if you ride in a group:\n\n- **Read the road, not the wheel.** Looking at the road two riders ahead lets you anticipate. Looking at the wheel in front means you react.\n- **Hold position through pulses.** When the pace surges briefly, don't let a gap open. Closing a gap costs three to five times more energy than holding the wheel.\n- **Climb where you'll finish.** If the group is going to split on a climb, position yourself where the split happens before it happens — not after.\n\nThese aren't fitness gains. They're efficiency gains. Same engine, more outcome.\n\n## The deeper sell\n\nLachlan Morton made a comment on the podcast that has stayed with me, and I'll borrow it because it sums up what *le metier* is really about:\n\n> \"Most people choose to drive a car. Most people choose to be overweight. Most people choose to not get outside in the fresh air. There's something countercultural in the essence of a bike.\"\n\nBeing a cyclist is already choosing differently. The community of serious amateurs you ride with is a community of people who have chosen something specific and harder than the default. That's worth remembering at Week 6, when the discipline of the system is starting to feel routine.\n\nYou're not just training. You're practising a craft. A craft has been there for a long time before any of us showed up, and it'll be there long after we're gone. The reason this module sits in the middle of the course is to remind you that the system is in service of the craft — not the other way around.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Skipping the mid-course check because the numbers are scary.** They're not scary. They're data. Run them honestly.\n\n**Using \"the craft\" as an excuse to skip the work.** Some riders read this module and use it as permission to dial back the training. That's not what it says. The craft is on top of the work, not instead of it.\n\n**Ignoring the joy factor.** If you're miserable on the bike, the system isn't working. Joy is a metric. Track it.\n\n**Doing a recovery week and a quality week at the same time.** This week is recalibration. Next week we ramp back up. Don't hit Tuesday's threshold session this week.\n\n## How this connects to Week 7\n\nYou've measured. You've reflected. You've recalibrated. Now we go back to the work — but at a higher level. Week 7 introduces periodisation properly: how to structure training in blocks that build, peak, and recover, rather than the same week repeated forever. This is where the back half of the course earns the front half.\n\nFor this week, take the pause seriously. Run a ride with no head unit data visible. Ride with humans. Reconnect with why you started. Then come back fresh on Monday and we ramp into the build phase.",
  "07-periodisation": "## What you've been doing wrong without realising\n\nMost amateurs do the same training week, month after month, and wonder why their FTP isn't moving. That isn't training. That's exercise. Training has direction. It builds. It progresses. It peaks.\n\nThat's periodisation, and it's the single biggest difference between how Dan Lorang builds a season for the riders he coaches at Red Bull–Bora–Hansgrohe and how most amateurs stumble through theirs. It's also the thing the cycling internet has actively confused — by selling you the idea that the same threshold workout repeated forever is \"structured training.\" It isn't. It's just a session schedule.\n\nThis module gives you the framework. By the end of the week, you'll know what phase you're in, what comes next, and how to time your training so you arrive at your target event in the form of your life — not the form of your last gran fondo.\n\n## Consistency first — Lorang's opening principle\n\nBefore we touch periodisation, Lorang's first principle:\n\n> \"If we talk about endurance sport, consistency is quite important. It's not about the one key session, about one big day. It's more about giving your body constantly a certain amount of load. And to realise this, load management is quite important — to avoid injuries, to avoid sickness, to avoid mental issues.\"\n\nConsistency over heroics. The reason periodisation works is that it gives you a structure that maintains consistency while progressing the load. Without periodisation, riders chase peak sessions and burn through their sustainability; with periodisation, the load builds and the body keeps absorbing it.\n\nSeiler reinforces this from a different angle, with one of the most quoted lines from the entire podcast archive:\n\n> \"I had a PowerPoint slide with a number on it like 532. This is a pretty important number what is it? And no, that's the number of sessions that athlete successfully achieved in that year. When athletes stand on the podium and you ask them what's your secret they'll say my secret is I don't have a secret. My secret is that I get the work done.\"\n\nFive hundred and thirty-two sessions. Across a year. That's the sport at the top — not the perfect interval, but the relentless accumulation of completed sessions. Periodisation is the architecture that lets you keep getting the work done.\n\n## The three-phase model — Base, Build, Peak\n\nJoe Friel's foundational work on periodisation breaks training into three phases. We're going to use a simplified version that works for cyclists with jobs, families, and 8 to 12 hour weeks.\n\n**Base.** The foundation. Lots of Zone 2. Strength is heavy. Intensity is minimal. Goal: build the aerobic engine, fix limiters, lay down volume the body can adapt to. Length: 8–16 weeks if you're starting fresh; 4–6 weeks at the start of a Roadman block.\n\n**Build.** The work that creates performance. Sweet-spot work, threshold intervals, structured high-intensity. Volume drops slightly to make room for the harder work. Strength shifts to maintenance. Goal: convert aerobic capacity into power at the duration that matters for your goal.\n\n**Peak.** The taper into the event. Volume drops further. Intensity stays sharp but reps drop. Strength drops to once a week. Goal: arrive at the start line fresh, sharp, hungry.\n\nFor The Roadman Method, you've spent Weeks 2–6 in a Base block. Week 7 starts the Build phase. Weeks 11 and 12 will be Peak / integration.\n\n## Mesocycles — the unit you actually plan in\n\nA periodised plan isn't a single linear arc. It's a series of three-to-four-week blocks called mesocycles. Each mesocycle has a structure:\n\n- **Week 1:** Loading week — moderate progression of volume or intensity\n- **Week 2:** Loading week — slightly higher than week 1\n- **Week 3:** Peak loading week — the hardest of the block\n- **Week 4:** Recovery week — volume drops 30–50%, intensity drops to almost zero\n\nThree weeks loading, one week recovery. Then the next mesocycle starts at a slightly higher absolute load — more volume, harder intervals, or longer race-pace work, depending on the phase.\n\nThis is the rhythm of the plan from here forward. You ran your first recovery week in Week 6 (mid-course pivot). Weeks 7, 8 and 9 are loading. Week 10 is a recovery week. Weeks 11 and 12 are taper into the post-course transition.\n\nIf you're following the Premium TrainingPeaks plan, this structure is already built. If you're building your own using the Season Planning Template, lock in the 3:1 rhythm first. Don't let the loading phase drift to four weeks. The recovery week is non-negotiable.\n\n## What changes in the Build phase\n\nPractical week shape, this week:\n\n**The long ride stays.** Duration is still doing the heavy lifting in early Build. Don't shorten it. If anything, extend by 15–30 minutes if your bracket allows.\n\n**One sweet-spot session enters the week.** Sweet-spot work — 88–94% of FTP — is the first proper intensity work we've added. It's the highest aerobic load with the lowest neuromuscular cost, which is why it's the natural opener for the Build phase. The session in the workbook is 3 × 10 minutes at sweet-spot, building to 3 × 15 over the next two weeks.\n\n**Strength stays at two sessions, stable load.** Don't increase the gym work as cycling intensity goes up. Hold maintenance.\n\n**Recovery rides do not get harder.** This is where most riders sabotage themselves. They add intensity to the hard sessions and accidentally add intensity to the easy sessions. Keep the easy days easy. The polarised principle holds even harder when the hard work gets harder.\n\n**Sleep gets MORE protected.** As intensity goes up, the recovery cost goes up. The 7.5–8 hour sleep target you've been working on becomes non-negotiable.\n\n## Heart rate vs power — Lorang's preference for the daily metric\n\nWhen the work gets harder, the question of how to control intensity gets more important. Lorang has a strong view here, and it's worth knowing why:\n\n> \"I'm honest, I'm a big fan of heart rate. If I would have to choose between one of both I would always take heart rate. Because heart rate is always the reaction of your body to something, to a load that you give. If you say the athlete you stay at 130 heart rate, that's still the same.\"\n\nHis point: heart rate integrates everything — fatigue, life stress, altitude, dehydration, illness, hormonal cycle. Power doesn't. If you blindly chase a power number on a day your body is at 80%, you do too much. If you use a heart rate cap, the body self-regulates.\n\nFor Build-phase intervals, the practical recommendation: prescribe by power, but cap by heart rate. If the prescribed sweet-spot is 240 watts but your heart rate climbs above your sweet-spot ceiling within the first interval, you're tired. Drop the wattage 5–10% for the day. Don't grind through.\n\n## Periodise life, not just training\n\nLorang puts this on a level with periodising training, and it's an idea most amateurs ignore until it breaks them:\n\n> \"Share it with the people around you. When I coached age groupers — by sharing this is also a big motivation for them and it just takes people on board to your project. When you have stress with the family, with your girlfriend, your wife — this really has a big impact on your health, on how you feel, on your performance. So this is always something what you try to avoid if possible.\"\n\nLook at your next 12 weeks. Where are the high-stress life weeks? End-of-quarter at work. School holidays. A move. A wedding. Map them onto the training plan now. If a peak loading week falls on a high-stress life week, swap the order — load when life is calm, recover when life is loud.\n\nThe body keeps a single ledger. Plan accordingly.\n\n## What NOT to chase — Lorang on altitude camps\n\nWorth flagging because some riders in this audience will be tempted: altitude training is generally a bad bet for amateurs.\n\n> \"If you have not so much time, if you basically just have two weeks, then in my opinion it makes more sense to go two weeks in the south making a proper training camp. In altitude there are so many things what you can do wrong that there's a high risk that the performance benefit will not be there.\"\n\nA two-week training camp at sea level — in good weather, with company, eating well, riding consistently — will produce more performance gain for almost every amateur than a two-week altitude block where you're more likely to under-recover, under-fuel, and travel home flat.\n\nIf you have a window for a training camp during this course, plan it. Sea level. Good food. A pile of rides. Don't chase altitude.\n\n## Common mistakes in the Build phase\n\n**Adding too much intensity at once.** The temptation is to add sweet-spot AND threshold AND VO2 in the same week because the legs feel good. Don't. We add one new intensity per mesocycle.\n\n**Skipping the recovery week.** This is the single most predictable failure point of any periodised plan. The recovery week feels like wasted time when you're flying. It isn't. It's where the adaptation lands.\n\n**Letting the long ride drift to \"moderate.\"** The long ride is still Z2. Don't accidentally turn it into a third quality session because you're feeling sharp.\n\n**Ignoring the readiness check.** This is when the morning numbers matter most. If RHR is up, sleep is short, and legs feel heavy — back off. The plan flexes around the body, not the other way round.\n\n**Building backwards from a target without backwards-counting the recovery weeks.** If your event is 8 weeks out, count the mesocycles backwards. Don't squeeze a 12-week plan into 8 weeks by skipping recovery.\n\n## How this connects to Week 8\n\nYou've added intensity. Volume is staying high. The work is starting to compound, and the body composition question is going to come up again — especially if you're aiming at a hilly event in the second half of the course. Next week we tackle race weight directly, without apology and without falling into the diet-culture traps. The same fuelling principles from Week 3, applied with more precision.\n\nThe full Friel-Lorang periodisation framework — what it looks like across a full season, not just a 12-week block — is in [Cycling Periodisation: The Friel, Lorang and Johnson Approaches](/blog/cycling-periodisation-friel-lorang-johnson).\n\nStay with the architecture. Run the mesocycle. Trust the structure.",
  "08-race-weight": "## The conversation nobody's having\n\nBody composition is the hidden universal motivator in cycling. Almost every serious amateur in this audience thinks about it. Very few talk about it openly. And the approaches most cyclists take — restricting calories, skipping meals, fasted rides — are precisely the strategies that tank performance, destroy recovery, and lead to the 9pm fridge raids that undo everything.\n\nThis module addresses race weight head-on, without apology. Not through restriction. Through precision. The same principles that took me from 86kg to 79kg in 12 weeks while eating more food than I'd ever eaten — applied at the right phase of the training year, with the right protections in place.\n\nBefore anything else: you don't have to do this block. Some riders shouldn't. We'll talk about who should and who shouldn't. If you're already lean for your build, the right answer this week is to read the module, take the principles, and skip the deficit. If you're carrying weight that's costing you watts on the climbs, this is the week to start moving it — but slowly, deliberately, and never at the cost of training quality.\n\n## The first decision — should you be doing this at all?\n\nThree questions:\n\n**1. Is this a Build phase or a Peak phase?** Body composition work belongs in the Build phase or transition periods. You don't chase weight in the four weeks before a peak event — you protect performance instead. We're in late Build. The window is narrowing but still open.\n\n**2. Are your training and sleep dialled?** A nutrition deficit on top of broken sleep and inconsistent training is a recipe for hormonal problems, especially in male riders over 40 and female riders under-fuelled. If sleep is below 7.5 hours and training is irregular, fix those first. Body composition can wait.\n\n**3. Is the gap meaningful?** If you're already at 8–10% body fat as a male rider or in the same proportional range as a female rider, the marginal gain on the bike from another two kilos lost is small and the cost in performance is high. Not every rider should chase race weight. The riders who benefit most are the ones still 5+ kilos above their ideal — usually with body fat in double digits.\n\nIf three yeses, this block is for you. If a no, take the principles forward without the deficit.\n\n## Why \"eat less, ride more\" fails\n\nDunne is precise about why the popular formula doesn't work for serious cyclists, and the answer matters.\n\n> \"From both male and female athletes, there are some serious metabolic implications of not just underfuelling on an absolute level but actually when we start to look at the distribution of it throughout the course of the day. You can cause this additional stress where you have significant hours of being in this chronic deficit where your body is not getting enough fuel to maintain normal physiological processes.\"\n\nThe body works on what physiologists call a law of preservation. When the daily energy availability drops below a threshold — roughly 30 calories per kg of fat-free mass per day in the IOC's RED-S framework — the body starts shutting things down. Recovery suppresses. Hormones drop. Bone density declines. Immune function weakens. Performance plateaus or regresses.\n\nThe cyclist who wakes up, has a coffee, rides three hours fasted, eats a small lunch, smashes a Tuesday threshold session in the evening, and has a \"light\" dinner is putting themselves in chronic deficit for sixteen hours a day. The scale might come down for a few weeks. The performance comes down with it. And then the rebound comes — usually around weeks six to eight — when the body's preservation mechanisms force a binge cycle the rider can't control.\n\nWe're not doing that. We're doing precision deficits.\n\n## The Roadman approach — deficit windows\n\nThe principle: most days, you're at maintenance or slight deficit. Hard days, you're at maintenance or surplus. Long ride days, you fuel the work. Recovery days, you eat slightly less. The deficit averages out across the week without ever being dangerous on any single day.\n\nIn practice:\n\n**Easy / off days:** 200–300 kcal below maintenance. This is the deficit window. Carbs taper at dinner. Protein stays high. Fibre and vegetables fill the volume.\n\n**Quality / hard days:** Maintenance. The session needs the fuel.\n\n**Long ride days:** Maintenance or slight surplus. Fuel the ride properly (use the targets from Week 3) and refuel post-ride. Don't try to \"save calories\" by under-fuelling the long ride. You'll bonk and binge.\n\n**Recovery weeks:** Maintenance throughout. Don't stack a deficit on top of reduced training — the body is already trying to absorb adaptation. Add deficit pressure and you'll erase the gain.\n\nAcross a week with three quality days, three easy days, and one off day, this typically averages out to roughly 1,000–1,500 kcal of weekly deficit. That's about 200g of body fat per week — half a pound. Slow. Sustainable. Protects training. Doesn't trigger the preservation response.\n\nA 12-week block at this rate moves you 5–8kg without ever feeling deprived. That's the maths that worked for me. It works for the riders we coach because it doesn't ask anyone to white-knuckle through hunger.\n\n## Protein is non-negotiable\n\nIf you take one number from this module, take this one: 1.8–2.2 grams of protein per kilo of body weight per day. Every day. Especially on deficit days.\n\nWhy protein matters more in a deficit:\n\n- It's the most satiating macronutrient — keeps you full at lower calories\n- It's the most thermogenic — your body burns more calories digesting it\n- It protects muscle mass when you're in deficit (you lose fat, not lean tissue)\n- It supports recovery from training that doesn't stop because you're cutting weight\n\nDistribute it across four meals. Breakfast 30g (eggs, Greek yoghurt, protein shake — front-load it). Lunch 30–40g. Snack or post-training 25–30g. Dinner 30–40g. Most riders we work with are eating fine total protein but most of it at dinner. Spread it.\n\n## Sarah Berry's free intervention, again\n\nThe meal-order trick from Module 3 matters more in a deficit phase. Same meal, lower glucose peak, slower gastric emptying — meaning you stay full longer on the same calories.\n\nEat the salad first. The protein next. Then the carbs. Five seconds of behaviour change. Smoother energy through the day, less hunger between meals, easier deficit to sustain.\n\n## The alcohol audit\n\nThe single biggest body composition lever for most cyclists in this audience isn't food — it's alcohol. Three pints on Friday night plus two beers on Saturday plus a glass of wine on Sunday is roughly 1,500 calories a week with almost zero satiety, plus a nightly disruption to the deep sleep that drives recovery.\n\nYou don't have to go dry. But for the next four weeks, audit it. Know what you're drinking and when. Cut the weekday units to zero. Keep the social weekend if it matters to you. Most riders find dropping weekday alcohol alone produces visible body composition movement within three to four weeks without changing anything else.\n\nIf the social piece is important — and Lachlan Morton would tell you it is — protect the Saturday post-ride pint and lose the Tuesday weeknight glass. The trade is favourable.\n\n## The decision tool, every meal\n\nApply the same training-to-train versus training-to-perform distinction from Module 3. Add one more layer for body composition work:\n\n**Training to perform** (race-pace efforts, key sessions, long rides) — fuel hard. Don't deficit. Don't experiment. Performance days are not weight loss days.\n\n**Training to train** (most Z2, steady aerobic work) — fuel adequately. Carbs in, but moderate. Don't bonk, but don't surplus either.\n\n**Off / recovery days** — gentle deficit window. Protein high. Carbs lower. Vegetables and fibre to fill the plate.\n\nThree buckets. Three different fuelling profiles. Apply them every meal this week.\n\n## The metric that matters — and the one to ignore\n\nThe number to track: weekly average weight, taken at the same time of day for seven consecutive days, then averaged. Not daily weight. Daily weight bounces 1–2kg with hydration and gut content; weekly average shows the trend.\n\nThe other metrics:\n\n- Waist measurement, fortnightly. Tape doesn't lie.\n- Photos every four weeks. The mirror lies; photos don't.\n- Performance numbers — power output on a stable benchmark session. If watts hold or improve while weight drops, you're doing it right. If watts drop, the deficit is too aggressive.\n\nIgnore: the daily scale number. The number on social media. The \"ideal\" race weight calculator. Your weight relative to a pro who has different genetics and a different sport. None of those are useful inputs.\n\n## What you should be feeling\n\nIf the deficit is calibrated correctly:\n\n- Energy stays even\n- Sleep stays solid\n- Hunger appears about an hour before meals — not all day\n- Training quality holds\n- Mood is stable\n\nIf you're experiencing:\n\n- Constant hunger\n- Poor sleep\n- Crashing afternoon energy\n- Power dropping on stable benchmark sessions\n- Mood swings or persistent low motivation\n\nThe deficit is too big. Bump up to maintenance for a week. Reset. Restart at a smaller deficit. This is not a competition with yourself. The slowest rider to lose the weight wins.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Going too hard, too fast.** Aggressive cuts produce rebounds. The 200–300 kcal deficit on easy days only is the floor of what works and the ceiling of what's safe.\n\n**Cutting carbs across the board.** Carbs aren't the enemy. Untimed carbs are the enemy. Move them around the day, don't eliminate them.\n\n**Skipping the long ride fuelling.** Under-fuelling the long ride is the fastest way to wreck the entire deficit block. Fuel the ride, deficit the dinner.\n\n**Stacking a deficit on top of broken sleep.** If you're under 7 hours, fix sleep first. Body composition cannot be rushed against a broken recovery foundation.\n\n**Comparing yourself to a 24-year-old WorldTour rider.** They have different genetics, different load, different recovery, and different decade. Your race weight isn't theirs. Find your own.\n\n**Talking about it constantly.** The riders who chase race weight quietly tend to land it. The riders who post about it daily tend to stall.\n\n## How this connects to Week 9\n\nYou've added precision to the fuelling. The body composition is moving. Next week we go back to the bike and back to the gym for the second pass at strength — this time targeting power. The low-cadence torque work that John Wakefield prescribes to Red Bull–Bora–Hansgrohe riders. This is where the back half of the course produces the watts you came here for.\n\nFor the full case against the 'eat less, ride more' model — and the data on why it fails for cyclists specifically — [Body Composition for Cyclists: The Lighter-Faster Myth](/blog/body-composition-cyclists-lighter-faster-myth) has the detail.\n\nRun the deficit window if it's right for you. Skip it if it isn't. Either way, take the meal-order trick, the protein floor, and the alcohol audit forward. Those three alone change body composition for most cyclists without ever needing to chase a deficit.",
  "09-power-where-it-counts": "## Why this is the session that changes everything\n\nYou're nine weeks in. You've built the architecture, fuelled it, recovered properly, and run six weeks of strength work. Now we add the on-bike session that converts all of it into power on the road.\n\nI called this the session that changes everything in the video, and I meant it. Low-cadence torque intervals are the most misunderstood and most undertrained quality session in amateur cycling. The cycling internet has spent the last decade dismissing them as \"old-school\" or \"junk miles.\" Meanwhile, the coaches behind Tour de France yellow jerseys, World Championship medals, and Olympic results have been prescribing them for years.\n\nThe science finally caught up. A 2024 study in PLOS ONE compared low-cadence interval training (40–60 RPM) against freely chosen cadence intervals across an eight-week intervention. The low-cadence group improved VO2max by 8.7% versus 4.6% for the freely chosen cadence group. Maximum aerobic power improved 8.1% versus 3%. Same sessions, same total work, same effort — nearly double the result. The only difference: one number on the bike computer screen.\n\nThat study confirms what John Wakefield, Dan Lorang, Tim Kerrison and the other coaches in our network have been saying for years.\n\n## Why it works — the mechanism\n\nThree reasons low-cadence work outperforms freely chosen cadence at the same intensity.\n\n**1. Muscle fibre recruitment.** At 90 RPM, your aerobic muscle fibres (type 1) handle most of the load. They're efficient, they don't fatigue easily, and they don't need much oxidative capacity to do the work. At 50 RPM with the same wattage, the load per pedal stroke roughly doubles. Your type 1 fibres can't handle it alone — they recruit type 2a and type 2x fibres to help. Suddenly fast-twitch fibres are doing aerobic work, which forces them to develop more mitochondria, more capillaries, and more oxidative capacity. You're aerobicising the entire muscle, not just the fibres that already had aerobic capacity.\n\n**2. Neuromuscular pathway development.** The brain learns to recruit more muscle fibres per pedal stroke. That sounds abstract until you realise it means more force per stroke, at the same heart rate, for the same effort. Free watts.\n\n**3. Aerobic engine expansion without volume cost.** This matters for time-crunched riders. You can build the same aerobic adaptation in less total training time, because the per-minute stimulus is higher. Most amateurs in this audience can't add another five hours of Z2 to the week — but they can swap one easy ride for a torque session and produce more adaptation than the volume would have done.\n\n## The previous research and why it was wrong\n\nOlder studies on low-cadence training failed to find clear benefit. There's a reason. As I covered in detail on the podcast, studies like Christopherson 2014, Nimmer 2012, Luda 2016 and Witty 2016 used protocols too short, loads too light, and cadence ranges too high to produce the stimulus. It's like testing weightlifting with 2kg dumbbells and concluding lifting doesn't work.\n\nThat 2024 PLOS ONE study used real protocols — eight-week intervention, four-minute intervals, real intensity, sustained loads. The result is clear. The earlier null findings were a measurement problem, not a training problem. The coaches working with the best riders in the world knew this all along — they kept prescribing the sessions and waited for the science to catch up.\n\nThis is one of the cleaner examples of what I've called the science-versus-coaching tension on the podcast: respect the evidence, but don't dismiss accumulated coaching wisdom because the evidence base is incomplete. Sometimes the science takes a while.\n\n## The two sessions — exactly what to do this week\n\n**Session 1: The torque interval (climb-based)**\n\nThe session John Wakefield prescribes to Red Bull–Bora–Hansgrohe riders, simplified for amateur use:\n\n- Find a climb with a sustained 4–7% gradient. You want the same gradient for every rep — not rolling, not flat, not super steep.\n- Warm up properly. 20 minutes building from very easy to high Z2.\n- Settle into the climb. Stay seated. Cadence 40–60 RPM. Effort RPE 7 out of 10 — hard but controlled, sustainable for the full duration.\n- Hold for 4 minutes.\n- Roll back down to recover for 4 minutes. Easy spin, around 90+ RPM, to flush the legs.\n- Repeat 4 times.\n- Cool down 15 minutes easy.\n\nTotal session time: roughly 80 minutes. Total quality work: 16 minutes at low cadence under load.\n\nForm notes: stay seated. Don't grind from the hips. Drive through the heel. Engage the core — if your lower back fires up, drop the gear one and re-engage your trunk. The Pallof press and dead bug work from the gym is exactly the trunk strength this session demands.\n\n**Session 2: Flatter sustained torque (later in the week)**\n\nOnce you've adapted to the climb-based session, add a longer flatter version:\n\n- Find a flat or false-flat road. No traffic, no junctions, no stops.\n- Warm up 20 minutes.\n- Push into a higher gear than you'd normally use for the wattage. Cadence 50–65 RPM. Effort RPE 6–7.\n- Hold for 8 minutes.\n- 4 minutes easy spin recovery.\n- Repeat 3 times.\n- Cool down 15 minutes.\n\nTotal session time: roughly 80 minutes. Total quality work: 24 minutes at low cadence.\n\nThe flatter version emphasises sustained recruitment over the brief peak load of the climb session. Use them in the same week, separated by at least 72 hours.\n\n## Where these sessions fit in the periodised plan\n\nWe're in the back half of the Build phase. Power development is the priority. Sweet-spot from Week 7 stays in the schedule. Torque enters now. By Week 10, the body composition work is mostly done and the focus is fully on race-pace performance work into the taper.\n\nThe week, this week:\n\n- **Long ride** (Z2) — duration extends if your bracket allows\n- **Torque Session 1** (climb-based) — quality day\n- **Recovery / easy ride**\n- **Sweet-spot session** (from Week 7) — maintained at 3 × 15 minutes\n- **Strength session 1** — Foundation/Build phase, hold load\n- **Torque Session 2** (flat) — quality day\n- **Strength session 2** — hold load, drop one set if cumulative fatigue is high\n- **Off day**\n\nThis is the highest cumulative load week of the course so far. Sleep gets the most protection of any week. The readiness check is non-negotiable. If the morning numbers say you're tired, swap a torque session for an easy spin. We are not grinding through fatigue this week.\n\n## Power profiling — know your limiter\n\nThe Power Profile Self-Assessment in your downloads asks you to test or estimate your power across four durations:\n\n- 5 seconds (sprint)\n- 1 minute (anaerobic capacity)\n- 5 minutes (VO2 max)\n- 20 minutes (threshold)\n\nPlotted relative to body weight, you'll see your \"shape.\" Riders typically fall into three rough categories:\n\n- **Diesel.** Strong sustained, weaker on the kicks. Threshold-strong, sprint-weak. Usually long-effort cyclists, gran fondo specialists, time trialists.\n- **Puncher.** Strong on 1–5 minute efforts, weaker on long sustained. Usually criterium and short-course riders.\n- **Sprinter.** Strong 5-second peak, weaker everywhere else. Track and bunch-finish specialists.\n\nMost riders in this audience are some flavour of diesel — and that's fine. But knowing your shape tells you what to train. A diesel doing constant threshold work is reinforcing a strength. Adding torque and short VO2 work fixes the limiter. A puncher doing short intervals only is the same trap in reverse.\n\nThe Premium plan has a personalised power-development block based on your profile. If you're running the Standard tier, the manual has guidance on which sessions to bias depending on which limiter you're addressing.\n\n## The compound interest of S&C and torque\n\nThe reason we sequenced strength in Week 4 and torque in Week 9 is that they compound. The Foundation Phase strength work has built the underlying force capacity. The torque intervals on the bike force the body to use that strength in pedalling-specific positions. Strength alone produces gym-strong cyclists. Torque alone produces under-strengthened riders chasing recruitment they don't have. Together — proper compound interest.\n\nTim Kerrison, when he was head of performance at Team Sky / Ineos, was one of the first coaches to make this case publicly. The team's domestiques, the ones doing the long pulls on the front of the bunch, weren't just aerobic monsters — they were strong. Heavy lifting in the off-season, torque work on the bike year-round. The result was riders who could hold high sustained power up gradients for 20+ minutes when it mattered.\n\nYou're doing a smaller version of that. The principles are the same.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Going too heavy on the gear.** Low-cadence work is uncomfortable, but it's not supposed to be a 1RM grind. If your knees are screaming or you're rocking in the saddle, the gear is too big. RPE 7 means hard but controlled.\n\n**Doing torque on a hill that's too steep or too rolling.** Find the right hill. Sustained gradient. No surges in cadence. If the only hill near you is 12%, find a different one or use the flat version.\n\n**Doing torque on broken legs.** This is a high-quality session. If you're in the deep fatigue end of the week, swap to easy. Quality over completion.\n\n**Stacking torque on top of strength on the same day.** Don't. Two quality sessions. Two strength sessions. Plus a long ride. Plus a sweet-spot. That's a full week. Don't compound them.\n\n**Letting the easy days drift.** With this many quality sessions in a week, the easy days have to be properly easy. The polarised principle is sharper now than ever.\n\n**Skipping fuelling on torque days.** These sessions cost. Fuel them at race-pace levels — 60–90g/h on the bike. This is a training-to-perform session, not a training-to-train session.\n\n## How this connects to Week 10\n\nYour power is developing. Your fitness is at its highest of the course. Now we address the factor that determines whether all of this fitness actually shows up when the road tilts up and the heart rate climbs and your brain starts asking you to stop. The mental game. Most cyclists give up before their body needs to. Next week we fix that.\n\nThe full coach interviews behind the low-cadence methodology — including the Red Bull–Bora–Hansgrohe session protocols — are in [Low-Cadence Training: What World Tour Coaches Prescribe](/blog/low-cadence-training-world-tour-coaches).\n\nHold the line this week. Run the torque sessions. Trust the legs that the strength work has built. The numbers are about to move.",
  "10-the-brain-is-the-limiter": "## Your biggest limiter isn't your legs\n\nYou've done the work. The training architecture is dialled. The fuelling is precise. The strength foundation is built. The power is developing. You're probably the fittest you've been in years — and now we have to address the factor that determines whether all of that fitness actually shows up on race day.\n\nIt isn't the legs. It's the head.\n\nYou know the moment. The road tilts up. You glance at the head unit and it starts showing those little red gradient bars ahead. The heart rate climbs. The breathing gets ragged. And before your body actually needs to stop, your brain tells you to back off. The wheel in front drifts away. Five minutes earlier you were strong. Now you're limit-state and saving for the rest of the climb that hasn't really started.\n\nThat moment is mental, not physical. Fixing it is often the fastest way to stop getting dropped — and it's the factor most training programmes pretend doesn't exist. This week we fix it.\n\n## The principle most coaches won't tell you\n\nPerformance physiology has a model called the Central Governor — first described by Professor Tim Noakes of the University of Cape Town — the idea that the brain regulates effort based on perceived risk to the body, and almost always pulls the brake before the body is properly in trouble. The brain holds back roughly 20–40% of available capacity as a safety buffer.\n\nYou can't override the governor through willpower alone. You can shift it. You can train it, just like you train aerobic capacity. Riders who consistently outperform their numbers — who finish events stronger than their FTP suggests they should — have done the work to push their perceived-effort threshold higher. They feel the same internal signals you do, but they've taught their brain to interpret them differently.\n\nThis module is the toolkit for doing that work.\n\n## Why amateurs collapse on climbs (when their numbers say they shouldn't)\n\nThree predictable patterns we see across the Roadman community.\n\n**1. Pacing without a plan.** Most amateurs head into a climb without a deliberate pacing strategy. They follow the wheel in front, react to the gradient, and discover their limit by exceeding it. The brain interprets the resulting metabolic distress as catastrophic and pulls hard on the governor.\n\n**2. Negative self-talk.** \"This is going to be brutal.\" \"I always blow up here.\" \"I'm not climbing well today.\" The brain takes these statements as direct instructions and pre-emptively pulls back. Self-talk is a coach voice — make sure the voice is on your side.\n\n**3. Treating suffering as a signal to stop.** Suffering is a feature of high-end aerobic work. It's also reliable, predictable, and survivable. Riders who panic at the onset of suffering give up the watts they came to spend. Riders who recognise the suffering, name it, and ride through it complete the work.\n\nWe're going to give you tools for all three.\n\n## The pre-ride mental preparation routine\n\nFive minutes, before any quality session this week. Not a meditation. A briefing.\n\n**1. Name the session.** Out loud or written: \"Today is 4 × 4 minutes torque on the climb. RPE 7. Sustainable. Hard but not maximal.\"\n\n**2. Visualise the difficulty.** Specifically. Not generally. Imagine the second interval — the one that always feels worst. Imagine your breathing getting harder. Imagine the brain saying back off. Imagine yourself holding the wattage anyway. Twenty seconds of deliberate visualisation.\n\n**3. Pre-load a single anchor phrase.** One sentence you'll say to yourself when the brain pulls. \"Stay in the box.\" \"This is what I trained for.\" \"Hold the wheel.\" Borrow one or write your own — but pick one and commit before the ride.\n\n**4. Set the technical conditions.** Heart-rate alarm on. Power cap if you have one. Bottle in the right cage. Phone away. The bike is set up to support the head.\n\n**5. Identify the meaning.** One sentence on why this session matters. Not motivational fluff — a real link to your goal. \"This session is what gets me through the climb at the gran fondo without blowing up at the bottom.\" Concrete reason.\n\nThat's it. Five minutes. Run it before every quality session this week.\n\n## The in-ride self-talk framework\n\nWhen the suffering hits, you have roughly three seconds before the brain commits to either fight or fold. The framework:\n\n**Name it.** \"OK, this is the hard part.\" Not \"I'm dying.\" Not \"I can't do this.\" Naming the experience neutrally separates you from the feeling. The feeling is happening. You are observing it.\n\n**Anchor.** Use the phrase you pre-loaded. Say it. Internally or out loud — out loud is often more powerful, even if you feel ridiculous on a quiet climb at 7am.\n\n**Chunk.** Don't think about the whole interval. Don't think about the whole climb. Count breaths. Count pedal strokes. \"Get to that next pylon.\" Break the suffering into three-pylon segments. The brain can handle three pylons. It panics at \"another four minutes.\"\n\n**Re-engage.** Posture check. Pull the elbows in. Drop the shoulders. Steady the breathing — five seconds in, five seconds out, even when it feels like you can't. The breath is the entry point to nervous-system regulation. Slow the breath, the brain follows.\n\nPractise this on a session you usually struggle with this week. You'll be surprised how much capacity you already had — you just hadn't trained the brain to access it.\n\n## The pacing plan — the document you write before, not during\n\nMost amateurs pace by reaction. The pros pace by plan. The difference is the document. Before any meaningful event or hard session this week, write the plan in three lines:\n\n- **The first third:** what wattage, heart rate or RPE you commit to no matter what the group around you does\n- **The middle third:** how you respond to the situation around you (move up, hold position, sit in)\n- **The final third:** when you're allowed to push (and what triggers the push)\n\nThree lines. Written. Before. The reason this works isn't the document — it's that the brain knows the plan and stops asking \"what should I do here\" mid-climb. Decision fatigue is real. The fewer in-the-moment decisions you have to make, the more energy stays in the legs.\n\nFor the next event on your calendar, write the plan this week. Use the Pacing Cards in your downloads — they have templates for climbs, time trials, group rides, and sportives.\n\n## Stress is real training load — the audit, again\n\nWe covered this in Week 5, but it lands differently in Week 10. By now you've done nine weeks of building. The cumulative load is meaningful. If your life stress has stayed flat, the body is absorbing the work. If life stress has climbed — work pressure, family event, financial worry — the body is paying for both at once.\n\nRun the Stress Audit this week. Write down the three biggest non-cycling stressors of the last fortnight. Map them onto the training calendar. If you're hitting peak training load on a peak life-stress week, something gives. Almost always, it's recovery, then performance, then health, in that order.\n\nDerek Teel was honest about it in our conversation:\n\n> \"I have three kids under four right now. I'm running my own business. There's days I leave and I'm so tense that I just need to breathe, I need to regulate my heart rate. I can't even think about having an effort right now.\"\n\nYou have permission to back off when life is heavy. The body doesn't separate sources of stress. Plan accordingly.\n\n## The role of meaning — what Lachlan got right\n\nLachlan Morton's interview keeps surfacing across this course because he gives us the framework for sustainability. The mental game isn't only about willpower in the moment. It's about why you're doing the work in the first place.\n\n> \"It's basically just trying to follow true motivations. Sometimes stepping back and looking around and being like, ah, I'm not actually enjoying this. I'm pursuing this for the wrong reason. What's something that would be exciting for me and motivating for me and going to get me out stoked on my bike every day?\"\n\nSelf-talk works when there's something true at the bottom of it. If you can't honestly say why you're suffering on a particular climb, the brain will pull the governor harder. Your why has to be present in your head when the suffering hits.\n\nFor the riders who took the Le Metier reflection in Week 6 seriously, this comes easier. You know why you ride. You know what the goal is connected to. The mental game gets easier when the meaning is clear.\n\n## The 3-2-1 race prep protocol\n\nIf you have an event in the next two weeks, run this:\n\n**3 days out.** Volume drops to 60% of normal. One short opener — 4–5 short surges of 30 seconds at race pace, well-recovered. Eat normally. Sleep 8 hours minimum.\n\n**2 days out.** Light spin only — 60 minutes Z1, no efforts. Carb-load slightly more than normal. Hydrate. Get to bed early.\n\n**1 day out.** 30–45 minute spin, very easy, with three short race-pace efforts of 30 seconds. The legs are reminded what fast feels like without taking any load. Keep the carbs high. Hydrate. Pre-pack the kit. Sleep.\n\n**Race morning.** Pre-event meal 2.5–3 hours out. Caffeine if you tolerate it, 60 minutes out. Warm up properly — at least 20 minutes building from easy to a few short race-pace efforts. Run the pre-ride mental routine.\n\nThe protocol is in your downloads with a checklist version for the bike bag.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Treating mental work as soft.** Every athlete you admire has a mental game. The riders who claim they don't are usually the ones who've internalised it deeply enough that they don't notice they're using it.\n\n**Skipping the pre-ride routine because it feels embarrassing.** Five minutes of deliberate preparation before every quality session, every event. Not optional.\n\n**Pacing by reaction.** Write the plan. Stick to it. Adjust by feel inside the framework, not outside of it.\n\n**Letting one bad ride contaminate the next.** The brain remembers blow-ups vividly. After a bad ride, write down two things you did well and one thing you'll do differently next time. Don't carry the loss into the next session.\n\n**Using \"the brain is the limiter\" as an excuse to ignore the body.** It's still real physiology. Adequate fuel, sleep and recovery underpin everything in this module. Mental tools are layered on top of a recovered, fuelled body — not a substitute for one.\n\n## How this connects to Week 11\n\nYou've now built every component of the system. Architecture. Fuelling. Strength. Recovery. Power development. Pacing and the mental game. Next week we put it all together and turn you from someone running a programme into someone who understands the system well enough to coach themselves. The integration module — week 11 — is the one that makes the value of this course permanent.\n\nIf you have an event in the next four weeks, [Cycling Taper and Race Preparation: The System That Works](/blog/cycling-taper-race-preparation-system) walks through the pre-race week in detail.\n\nRun the mental tools this week. Pick one climb that usually breaks you and ride it differently. Take the suffering, name it, ride through it. By next week the brain has new evidence — and that's how the governor gets retrained.",
  "11-your-system-integrated": "## What this week is for\n\nYou're at Week 11. The system is built. Architecture, fuelling, strength, recovery, power, mental game — every pillar has been addressed and you've lived inside the framework for ten weeks. Most courses end here. We don't, and the reason matters.\n\nThe hardest skill in this entire course isn't running an interval at the right power. It isn't fuelling the long ride. It isn't lifting a weight with good form. It's making smart decisions when life happens — when you miss a session, when you get a cold, when work blows up, when the legs feel weird and you don't know if it's fatigue or fitness. That's self-coaching, and that's what this week teaches.\n\nBy Sunday night, you should be able to run the next 12 weeks of training without us. You'll still want the community, the conversations, the updated plans — that's what Not Done Yet is there for. But the dependence on a programme telling you what to do next? That ends this week. You become the coach.\n\n## The five common mistakes self-coached athletes make\n\nBefore we build the toolkit, the diagnostic. Across our work with the community, five patterns come up over and over in self-coached riders:\n\n**1. Using AI for coaching decisions.** I'm going to take a clear position here. AI is useful for pulling reference data, comparing studies, and answering specific questions. It is not a coach. Coaching is a relationship — between an athlete and someone who knows their context, history, life pressures, and recovery patterns. The first three weeks of any new coaching engagement are the coach learning the athlete. AI doesn't have those three weeks. Don't use AI for the daily go/no-go call.\n\n**2. No periodisation.** Doing the same training week, month after month, without progression or recovery. We covered this in Week 7 — the cure is the mesocycle structure. Self-coached riders often default back to the same week because it's safer. Don't.\n\n**3. Ignoring recovery.** Training through fatigue, treating rest days as optional, never running a true recovery week. Week 5 covered this. The fix is running a 3:1 mesocycle structure and protecting recovery weeks like a religion.\n\n**4. Tracking the wrong metrics.** Obsessing over TSS, weekly hours, or strava-segment PRs while ignoring the metrics that actually predict adaptation: sleep average, intensity distribution, weekly RPE summary, body composition trend. Pick three to track. Ignore the rest.\n\n**5. Training alone without feedback.** No accountability, no second opinion, no feedback loop. The community exists for this reason — when you don't have a coach, you need other riders to mirror you. Post in the threads. Share the data. Ask the questions.\n\nIf any of those five patterns sound familiar, [Five Mistakes Self-Coached Cyclists Make (And How to Fix Them)](/blog/five-mistakes-self-coached-cyclists-make) is the standalone version of this diagnostic.\n\nIf two of those five describe you over the next three months, it's a flag. Self-coaching works when the discipline is internal. The framework below is how you build that discipline.\n\n## The weekly self-coaching review — 15 minutes, every Sunday\n\nThis is the single most important habit from this module. Block 15 minutes every Sunday for the rest of your cycling life.\n\nThe review covers six questions:\n\n**1. What did I actually do this week?** Pull the data. Not what was planned — what happened. List sessions, durations, average heart rate or power, RPE.\n\n**2. What was the intensity distribution?** Calculate green / yellow / red percentage. Compare to the polarised target.\n\n**3. How did I feel?** Sleep average, leg feel, motivation, mood. The subjective scale matters as much as the data.\n\n**4. What's the trend?** Compare this week to the last three weeks. Are the green-zone sessions getting easier at the same heart rate? Are the quality sessions producing the right power? Is body composition trending where it should?\n\n**5. What am I changing for next week?** Based on the trend, what's the adjustment? More volume? More recovery? Different session order? Be specific.\n\n**6. What's the one thing I'm watching?** Pick one thing — a single metric, a single sensation, a single behaviour — and watch it across the coming week. Not five. One.\n\nThat's the review. Fifteen minutes. The template in your downloads has the prompts and the space to write.\n\nThis is what professional coaches do for their riders. You're now doing it for yourself. The discipline of the review is the discipline of self-coaching.\n\n## The decision tree — common scenarios\n\nSelf-coaching is mostly making good decisions in unclear situations. The Decision Tree Cards in your downloads cover the most common ones. Some highlights:\n\n**Scenario: I missed today's session.**\n\n- Was it just one session, in an otherwise on-track week? → Skip it. Don't try to make it up. Move on.\n- Was it the long ride? → Push it to tomorrow if possible. If not, swap with the next quality session and slide the structure.\n- Has this been the third missed session in two weeks? → Audit. Something is off — work, sleep, motivation, or fitness. Don't keep grinding through.\n\n**Scenario: I'm getting sick.**\n\n- Symptoms above the neck (sniffle, mild sore throat) → Easy ride only. No quality work. Sleep extra.\n- Symptoms below the neck (chest, fever, body aches) → Off the bike. Full stop. Resume only when symptoms have been gone for 48 hours, then start at 50% load.\n\n**Scenario: Travel week.**\n\n- Plan for fewer hours. Pick two or three sessions you can realistically do. Keep one quality session and one long ride if possible. Strength becomes one bodyweight session.\n- Don't try to maintain normal volume. Travel is real life stress. Match the load to reality.\n\n**Scenario: My morning numbers are bad and a quality session is on the plan.**\n\n- Two days in a row of bad numbers → Swap to easy ride or rest. Don't push through.\n- One bad day after a hard session yesterday → Probably normal post-session fatigue. Trust the plan.\n- One bad day with no obvious cause → Run a 10-minute warm-up. If the legs come good, do the session. If not, swap to easy.\n\n**Scenario: I'm flying, the legs feel great, I want to do extra.**\n\n- This is the most dangerous self-coaching moment. Don't add load impulsively. The reason you feel great is the plan. Do the planned session, then go for a bonus easy ride if you must — never bonus quality.\n\nThese aren't exhaustive — the Decision Tree Cards give you more — but they're the situations that come up most. Memorise the principles.\n\n## When to back off — Lorang's two-interval rule, again\n\nThis is worth repeating because it's the single most useful real-time decision tool in coaching:\n\n> \"If you are doing these intervals and you can stay in that frame, you continue. But if you drop in the first two intervals already — the quality at that point — then we skip and you just make an easy run. And we reset the program.\"\n\nTwo intervals. If they're both off the prescribed power or RPE by more than 5%, you switch to easy. No grinding through. No \"I'll just do the last one.\" Save the legs. The body is telling you it can't make the adaptation today.\n\nThe hardest part of self-coaching is making this call without ego. There's no one else to give permission. You give it to yourself, you write down what happened, you move on.\n\n## Planning the next mesocycle — the post-course block\n\nThis week, plan your first post-course four-week mesocycle. Use the Mesocycle Builder Worksheet.\n\nThe plan needs five things:\n\n**1. The phase.** Is the next block another Build, a Peak into a goal event, or a Base re-set? Most riders coming out of this course will benefit from one more Build mesocycle (Weeks 13–16) before deciding whether to peak for an event or return to Base.\n\n**2. The progression.** What's getting harder, and how? Volume? Intensity? Specific sessions? Pick one variable to progress meaningfully.\n\n**3. The 3:1 structure.** Three loading weeks, one recovery week. Don't deviate.\n\n**4. The non-negotiables.** What stays the same? Strength twice a week. Long ride. Sleep target. Identify what holds while everything else flexes.\n\n**5. The single watched metric.** What's the one number you're tracking across this block? Pick it before you start, not retrospectively.\n\nThat plan, in your hands, is what you've earned in 11 weeks. Not a templated programme. A plan you understand.\n\n## What the Premium plan does this week\n\nPremium tier students submit their Mesocycle Builder Worksheet in the Not Done Yet thread for review. You'll get specific feedback on the structure — what's missing, what's likely to break, what to keep. This is one of the most useful moments in the entire course because the next 12 weeks of training are about to be informed by 12 weeks of data we both share.\n\nIf you're on Standard tier, post your plan in the community thread. The feedback loop matters — even informal feedback from other Roadman Method graduates outperforms going alone.\n\n## What \"becoming your own best coach\" actually means\n\nIt does not mean you don't need anyone else. It means:\n\n- You make the daily go/no-go calls without paralysis\n- You know which metrics matter and which to ignore\n- You recognise the patterns in your own physiology\n- You build plans you understand, not plans you copied\n- You ask better questions of better experts because you know what you don't know\n\nThe riders who stay sharp into their 40s, 50s and 60s aren't the ones with the most expensive coach. They're the ones who built durable self-coaching habits early and used coaches and communities to refine, not to replace.\n\nThat's what this week is for.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Skipping the weekly review because the week was unremarkable.** The unremarkable weeks are the ones the review compounds across. Every Sunday. Fifteen minutes. No exceptions.\n\n**Building the next mesocycle by copying the last one.** Progression. Pick one variable, move it.\n\n**Ignoring the watched metric because you don't trust it.** If you've picked the metric and you don't trust it, you picked the wrong metric. Pick a better one — but pick one.\n\n**Going back to AI for coaching decisions.** I've already said it. Don't.\n\n**Treating Week 12 as the finish line.** Week 12 is the start line for the next phase. The system continues. The community continues. The work continues.\n\n## How this connects to Week 12\n\nOne week left. Time to measure, reflect, and decide what comes next. Week 12 is the Exit Assessment — same six domains as Week 1, measured against your baseline. You'll see, in numbers, what twelve weeks of riding inside a system produced. Then we close the loop and look forward.\n\nFor this week, run the review. Plan the next mesocycle. Trust that the system is now yours. The training continues forever. The coaching, increasingly, comes from the rider you've spent eleven weeks becoming.",
  "12-not-done-yet": "## You did the work\n\nTwelve weeks ago you ran a difficult audit and put numbers on the page that probably weren't comfortable. You built the architecture, fuelled it properly, added strength, learned to recover, ran sweet-spot, dialled torque, addressed the head game, and learned to coach yourself.\n\nThis week we measure. Then we reflect. Then we look forward.\n\nThe course doesn't end here — the system does. What ends today is being a programme follower. What starts today is being a practitioner. The next 12 weeks, the next 12 months, the next decade of your cycling life — they happen on the back of the foundation you've laid in the past 84 days. The right thing to do this week is honour the work by measuring it honestly and planning what comes next with the same rigour.\n\n## The Exit Assessment\n\nSame six domains as Week 1. Same conditions if possible — same scale, same protocol, same time of day. The Exit Assessment Workbook is in your downloads.\n\n**1. Power baseline.** Retest. Same protocol you used in Week 1 if possible. Same warm-up. The number you produce here is meaningful — it's the answer to the question that brought you to the course in the first place.\n\n**2. Real training load review.** Pull the last 12 weeks. What was your actual completed volume? What was the intensity distribution? Compare to the four weeks before the course started.\n\n**3. Body composition.** Same scale, same conditions, same time of day. Measure waist, hip, weight. Take Week 12 photos for direct comparison with Week 1.\n\n**4. Sleep average.** What's been your real seven-night average for the past fortnight? Compare to your Week 1 baseline.\n\n**5. Subjective.** Energy, mood, motivation, leg feel — same 1–10 scales as Week 1.\n\n**6. Goal review.** Did you hit your primary 12-week goal? Your supporting goals? The single measurable target?\n\nDon't massage the numbers. The numbers are how we learn. If you didn't hit the target, the data tells you why — almost always it's an input (sleep, fuelling, stress, intensity discipline) rather than an effort problem. If you blew through the target, that's also data — it tells you what's possible from here.\n\n## What the data usually shows at Week 12\n\nAcross the riders we work with through this system, the typical Week 12 picture:\n\n- **Power up by 8–25 watts.** Smaller for riders who came in already well-trained. Larger for riders who came in with the most leaks.\n- **Body composition meaningfully shifted.** 3–6kg lower, with body fat percentage following.\n- **Intensity distribution holding above 70% green.** The polarised discipline has stuck.\n- **Sleep up 30–60 minutes from baseline.** The single biggest non-training intervention.\n- **Power-to-weight ratio improved by 5–12%.** This is the number that actually matters on the climbs, and it usually moves more than absolute power.\n- **Subjective scales up across the board.** Energy, mood, motivation, leg feel — riders feel different in their bodies, not just on the bike.\n\nIf three of those six describe your Week 12 numbers, the system worked. If five or six describe you, you're going to be hard to recognise from where you started.\n\nIf the numbers haven't moved as much as you'd hoped — that's also useful information. Twelve weeks is a meaningful block, but it's also a short block. Some adaptations take 6–12 months to compound. The system is what produces the long-term result. Twelve weeks is where the discipline became a habit. The watts come.\n\n## The reflection — five pillars, five paragraphs\n\nWrite three to five sentences per pillar. Honest. Rough. The 12-Week Review is a private document — write it for yourself.\n\n**Coaching.** What about my training architecture changed? What's the most important habit I'll carry forward? What did I get wrong in the first four weeks?\n\n**Nutrition.** What's the change in my relationship with food? Where am I still leaking? What fuelling rule is going to stay non-negotiable?\n\n**Strength.** Did I do the work? What did the gym give me on the bike? What's the plan for keeping it through the next 12 weeks?\n\n**Recovery.** Has my sleep changed? My stress management? What's the recovery habit I almost gave up but kept, and what did it produce?\n\n**Le Metier.** Has my relationship to riding changed? Am I enjoying it more? Less? Why? What's the next ride I want to do — not the next race, the next ride.\n\nThese aren't journal prompts to be precious about. They're the questions you'd ask a rider you coached. Now you're asking them of yourself, because you are the rider you coach.\n\n## The 90-Day Forward Plan\n\nThe template in your downloads asks you to commit to five things for the next 90 days:\n\n**1. The training phase.** Continue Build? Move into a Peak for a target event? Drop into a Base re-set? Pick one and own it.\n\n**2. The mesocycle structure.** Three weeks loading, one week recovery, repeat. Three mesocycles in 90 days, with a four-day buffer for travel, illness, life. Lock the dates in the calendar.\n\n**3. The non-negotiables.** Strength twice a week. Sleep target. Long ride. The polarised discipline. List the things that hold no matter what.\n\n**4. The one variable to progress.** Pick one. Don't pick five. Volume, threshold work, climbing-specific sessions, race-pace fuelling tolerance — one thing to deliberately progress.\n\n**5. The metric you watch.** One. Pick it. Watch it for 90 days. Don't change it because of a bad week.\n\nWrite it. Print it. Stick it on the wall by the bike. The plan you can see is the plan you keep.\n\n## What the system actually changed\n\nMost riders who come through this course don't tell us about the watts when they finish. They tell us about something else.\n\nThey tell us they've stopped second-guessing every session. The relief of that — the mental space recovered from not constantly wondering whether they're doing the right work — is what they didn't expect when they signed up.\n\nThey tell us they're sleeping better. They tell us they're not as wrecked between rides. They tell us their kids have noticed they're in a better mood on weekends.\n\nThey tell us they've started enjoying the ride again. The pressure of \"I have to make this hour count\" softens into \"I trust the architecture.\" The training becomes a frame for the riding, not a substitute for it.\n\nThese are real outcomes from real riders, and they're the ones that compound into the next decade of cycling. The watts are downstream. The system change is upstream.\n\n## The closing argument — Lachlan Morton, one more time\n\nI keep coming back to Morton because he's the rider I'd want every Roadman Method graduate to think about when they're deciding what comes next:\n\n> \"I had this realisation — this experience that I've had, five days with some mates out in the hills, just doing 100ks a day and then stopping and having a beer at night. I was like, this is the actual experience that people would enjoy riding. That needs to be part of what I do in terms of promoting the sport. Because you can always go bigger and longer or faster — but ultimately it's like, what are we trying to get out of bikes? We want people to get out there, be healthy, but just enjoy it for what it is.\"\n\nThe science on maintaining and building performance into your 40s and 50s — from masters athletes who are getting faster, not slower — is in [Cycling After 40: The Science of Getting Faster](/blog/cycling-after-40-faster-science).\n\nPerformance serves enjoyment. Not the other way around. The system you've built over 12 weeks gives you the tools to get faster — but the goal of getting faster is to enjoy the riding more. Faster up the climb you're attempting next year. Stronger on the long ride you've been wanting to do. Fitter at 50 than you were at 40, and still in the saddle at 60. The watts are in service of the ride.\n\nThat's what *Not Done Yet* actually means. The course has finished. The riding hasn't. The next chapter is already starting.\n\n## Where Not Done Yet comes in\n\nYou're going to need a community of serious riders who've been through the same system. The course gives you the knowledge. The community gives you the accountability, the feedback loop, and the people who actually understand what you've been doing for the past 84 days.\n\nThe Not Done Yet community is where this continues. Weekly live calls with me. Updated TrainingPeaks plans. Masterclasses with the experts. A community of riders at all stages of the system. If you've gone through The Roadman Method and want to keep sharpening — that's where it happens.\n\nI'm not going to hard-sell this. The course has been the value. The community is the next chapter for the riders who want one. The link is in your account page. Use it or don't — but if you do, you'll find a few hundred riders who've been exactly where you are right now and are happy to help.\n\n## Common mistakes this week\n\n**Skipping the Exit Assessment because you don't think the numbers will be impressive.** The numbers don't have to be impressive. The honesty has to be. Run the assessment. The data is for you.\n\n**Treating Week 12 as the finish line.** It's not. It's a checkpoint. The next mesocycle starts Monday.\n\n**Comparing your numbers to someone else's.** Your Week 1 to your Week 12 is the only comparison that matters. Not your buddy's Week 12. Yours.\n\n**Letting the discipline lapse the day the course ends.** The system works because it's continuous. The Sunday review continues. The architecture continues. The non-negotiables continue.\n\n**Forgetting why you started.** Go back and read the Week 1 audit. Read the goal you wrote down. Notice how far you've come from the rider who wrote that. Then plan the next 12 weeks for the rider you are now.\n\n## What I want you to take from these 12 weeks\n\nThree things, if you take only three.\n\nThe first: trust the easy work. The single most counter-intuitive idea in the entire system is that going slower most of the time is what builds the engine that lets you go faster when it matters. Every coach we've had on the podcast says it. The data backs it. Trust it for another 12 weeks and the next 12 watts come for free.\n\nThe second: the system is more important than any single session. You'll have bad weeks. Bad sessions. Weeks where life eats the schedule. The system survives. The discipline of the weekly review, the mesocycle structure, the recovery week — those are what hold across imperfect weeks. The session you nailed yesterday matters less than the architecture you ran for the past three months.\n\nThe third: you're not done yet. Whatever brought you to this course — a target event, a feeling that there was more in the legs, a frustration with stalled progress — you've answered some of it but not all of it. The work that produced these 12 weeks of progress is the same work that will produce the next 12 weeks, and the next, and the decade after that. The version of you that finished Week 1 wouldn't recognise the version that finished Week 12. The version that finishes Year 5 won't recognise this one either.\n\nWelcome to the rest of the riding. The map is yours. The compass is yours. The system is yours.\n\nYou're not done yet — and now you know exactly what to do next.\n\n---\n\n*Anthony Walsh, Roadman Cycling*",
};
